window.HIVEMIND_PRD = {
 "docs/prd/00-system.md": "# System overview\n\n> Status: foundation frozen except final verdict and pull mode. Universe, classification, Config, dispatch, extraction and human-review intake froze on October 18, 2026; the library and matching labels on October 20; the full review completed October 22. D149 adds extraction-time grouping, not yet integrated into the default pipeline. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Open system PRD. Recorded decisions take precedence over this document.\n\n## Purpose\n\nMaintain a thesis library: create theses, update their judgements and close them when due. Upstream continuously supplies classified information. Extraction reads each whole item as a senior hedge-fund analyst, alongside existing theses, and returns linked evidence and candidates with research-group associations, declaring new groups where needed. The library preserves those associations and compares identity: merge, succession, new thesis or skip; repeat, replace or new evidence. Each thesis has a question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due.\n\nD149 requires organisation during full-text reading, with associations included in extraction output. See [the library's grouping contract](06-views.md#research-groups-associate-during-extraction) and [extraction's responsibility](05-extraction.md#determine-associations-during-extraction). Rubin shipments and SK factory deployment are separate questions in one continuing business process. Default integration is pending; the [improvement plan](../poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md) records implementation gaps, D155 identity changes and validation work.\n\n## Why rebuild\n\nThe old system pulled dated batches. Category definitions existed in nine modules, configuration used five overriding layers, and time had several clocks. Explaining an item's path required reading multiple implementations. The rebuild gives each decision one owner.\n\n## System boundary\n\n- **Upstream, outside this system:** acquire material, classify it, identify related subjects and publication time, and deliver it. Acquisition details are deferred.\n- **This system:** dispatch through the library, final verdict and pull mode.\n- **Tests:** stream stored items in publication order through the product's path, with replay time supplied to pull mode.\n\n```text\nUpstream → Dispatch → Extraction → Thesis library ↔ Matching labels\n                         │               ↑\n                  read-only; groups   Pull mode: scheduled review,\n                                      freeze and close due theses\nFinal verdict: earlier draft; decide its role with pull mode\n```\n\n| From | To | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Research universe | Upstream | Subjects and tracked categories |\n| Research universe | Extraction | All themes for candidate selection in block 3 |\n| Config | Extraction, library, final verdict | Model settings version; library search result count |\n| Library | Extraction | Open subject theses and their dependencies: question, focus, due date and links; available groups under the library contract, in block 5 |\n| Library | Matching labels | Comparison records for human labels and future smaller-model training |\n| Dispatch, extraction, library | Human review | Unresolved items, proposed themes and repeated failures |\n\n## Two modes\n\n| | Incoming stream | Pull mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trigger | An item arrives | Day-end or scheduled interval |\n| Initiator | Upstream delivery | System scheduling |\n| Work | Read evidence/candidates and associations, preserve associations while matching identity, rejudge changed inputs under library rules | Overall review/update; freeze and close due theses; remaining design deferred |\n| Calendar | Never reads it | Sole time-driven module; receives “now” explicitly |\n\n## Principles\n\n1. Start from theses. Their needs determine what to read and retain; do not build a financial-number warehouse first.\n2. A thesis is a judgement; evidence records who said what in which item. Only incoming information creates evidence. Theses cannot prove each other.\n3. Retain sources for every thesis, evidence item and information-triggered change, including the location in the original.\n4. The stream is driven by incoming information, not the calendar. No input means no stream work; pull mode owns time.\n5. Dispatch sequence determines order. Read earlier sequences plus the current round's own writes, with results equivalent to serial processing. Append knowledge without rewriting history. Log obsolete late statements; accept newly learned matters with their actual dates.\n6. The research universe alone chooses subjects and categories. Upstream supplies only that scope; process every supplied item.\n7. At matching time, open one thesis per subject and focus, with separate current/next-year numeric periods; manual reopening is excluded. D155 governs the POC's bounded code-owned identity. Search finds comparisons, not final identity. Resolve an evidence item's thesis before searching within it.\n8. Fix questions and ladders in advance, and freeze the state when due. Rungs are verifiable outcomes. Append records and retain closed theses and replaced evidence.\n9. Do not guess unresolved identifiers or rules; use human review.\n10. Do not copy whole source material into the library. Retrieve it from the team's source store by ID.\n\n## Glossary\n\nUse one term per concept in the maintained design. `docs/check.py` checks terminology in the relevant page data and PRDs. Historical quotations, old source records and identifiers keep their original text. English replaces the previous Chinese presentation under D163; it does not rename stored identities.\n\n| Term | Meaning | Avoid |\n|---|---|---|\n| Research group | Theses organised around a continuing business process; association is decided during extraction under the library contract | Treating a group as an overarching investment thesis |\n| Admission reason | The one of four reasons a candidate thesis may open (D184): a main driver, an industry aggregate, a policy stage, a binding obligation; the answer states it as opens_as | Opening reason |\n| Item | One upstream unit processed by classification, dispatch and extraction | Using file for every kind of item |\n| File | Text-form source such as earnings, a report or news; qualify filesystem files as data/configuration files | Confusing source form with category |\n| Table data | Fixed-field information; one release, such as a 13F or statistical release, is one item | Treating every cell as an item |\n| Source material | Raw upstream material before classification | Calling excluded material an invalid item automatically |\n| Form | Text or table | Using it for genre |\n| Genre | Transcript, notes, memo, report, slides, article, letter or post | — |\n| Filing | Formal submission to a regulator, exchange or court; SEC filing where applicable | SEC document as a category |\n| Filing code | 10-K, 8-K, DEF 14A, etc. | Table code |\n| Category | One of T1–T16 | Category type |\n| Classification result | A category, duplicate merge, not source material, or invalid | Assuming every item has a category |\n| Metadata | Supplied classification result, form, subjects, publication time and other attributes | — |\n| Related subject | Company, cryptocurrency, commodity or theme identified for the item; it need not be in our universe | Equating company with stock |\n| Subject identifier | Typed CIK, ticker, registered company name, cryptocurrency/commodity symbol or theme ID | One untyped subject code |\n| Author | The party responsible for content; distinguish named individual and speaker | Treating delivery channel as author |\n| Author type | Company, media, analyst, official or individual; author table then channel default | Category type |\n| Publication time / publication day | Public-release timestamp / its New York date | Receipt time as publication day |\n| Upstream ID / sequence number | Stable upstream version identity / dispatch-owned arrival order | Dispatch serial number |\n| Original item | The item a duplicate merges into | — |\n| Invalid | A classification result with a reason | Mixing it with excluded source material |\n| Human review pool | Unresolved matters awaiting a person | Unqualified review pool |\n| Dataset / data interface | Registered collection of table data / access channel | Confusing either with category |\n| Prompt template / prompt / upload template | Shared five-block structure / assembled category instructions / upload table format | Unqualified template |\n| Role | Senior hedge-fund analyst in prompt block 1 | Identity, which names a record or subject |\n| Thesis / candidate thesis | An immutable question, ladder and due date / extraction's proposed uncovered question, including personal-memo judgements | Calling every quoted opinion a thesis; naked candidate where ambiguous |\n| Opinion | An author's, analyst's or media commentator's view | Confusing it with a stored thesis |\n| Statement | What a source party says | — |\n| Asset thesis / theme thesis | Tradable subject with business-impact rungs / theme with outcome-labelled rungs | Company view, object view, theme view |\n| Focus | The quantity and year or event the thesis concerns, alongside its subject | Thesis driver |\n| Ladder / rung | Exhaustive, non-overlapping outcomes: five, or two for yes/no | Fixed five-rung ladder; old verification-condition design |\n| Due date | When the debate can resolve; a number's publication day | Expiry bucket |\n| State | Our current rung judgement; not yet judged before a memo thesis first receives evidence | A second parallel state |\n| Conclusion | State plus reasons; may change through key facts/numbers even within one rung | Equating changed conclusion with changed score only |\n| Freeze | Preserve the current state and reasons at the due date, then close | A separate confirmed/falsified verdict in the current design |\n| Unavailable answer / pending publication | Proposed pull-mode cases, not yet approved | Treating proposals as implemented states |\n| Evidence tier | Fact, party statement, third-party view or rumour; only facts can establish the outcome | Mixing tiers with thesis kinds |\n| Link | Evidence linked to a thesis, with its judgement-specific push | Treating push as intrinsic to evidence |\n| Theme block | The open, judged theme theses shown to an asset thesis's judgement, one to all; the judgement states each theme's push on it | Theme context, macro block, theme dependency |\n| Search / comparison list | Find plausible matches / top N shown to matching | Treating search ranking as identity |\n| Open / closed | The lifecycle switch; only open theses update; closed theses remain searchable | Active thesis, inactive thesis |\n| Repeat / replace / new evidence | Same existing statement / a later statement superseding it / another event, period or speaker | Counting repeated references as independent evidence |\n| Replaced | Historical evidence no longer in force, kept for traceability | Deleted evidence |\n| Dependency | A thesis reads another's conclusion with a specified direction; no cycles | Premise in the current design |\n| Succession / chain | Next period of the same focus / its ordered sequence; evidence replacement also forms chains | Confusing succession with dependency |\n| Matching / matching record | Decide destinations and retain inputs, answers, checks and writes | Inferring identity solely from similar wording |\n| Merge | A candidate joins an open thesis or a part of it, inheriting its ladder and due date | Rewriting the target's identity |\n| Source reference | Upstream ID, category, publication day, location and quote/cell | — |\n| Original source | Who first said it and when; used for independence and replacement ordering | Counting each retelling independently |\n| Standalone summary | Evidence readable without its source; used by matching and rejudgement | — |\n| Duplicate check | Proposed scheduled consolidation of duplicate open theses; not yet approved | Treating it as implemented |\n| Rejudgement / round | Recompute from evidence in force and dependencies / all changes from one item | Incremental score arithmetic |\n| Matching labels / smaller model | Human-labelled destinations / future faster model trained from them | Assuming labels already provide acceptance |\n| Code | Programmed rules, distinct from model judgement | — |\n| Family / group | Five classification families / twelve theme-library groups | Calling theme groups categories |\n| Frozen design | A decided module version; changes need a new decision | Confusing design freeze with a frozen thesis state |\n\n## Modules\n\nSee the [PRD index](README.md) for files and status. The universe defines scope; classification labels; Config selects settings; dispatch routes; extraction reads; the library matches, stores and rejudges; matching labels collect supervision; pull mode owns time; human review receives unresolved matters. Final verdict remains an earlier draft.\n\n## Compared with the old system\n\n| Aspect | Old system | Rebuild |\n|---|---|---|\n| Input | Date-driven pulls | Incoming upstream items |\n| Processing | Daily batches | Item sequence order |\n| Categories | Separate definitions in nine modules | One set of 16 |\n| Scope | Overriding configuration, arguments and presets | Research universe |\n| Time | Several clocks | Publication day in the stream; supplied “now” in pull mode |\n| Sources | Local copies | Retrieve from team storage by ID |\n| Reports | Repeated downloads and stored individual numbers | Whole-item reading into theses and evidence |\n| Theses | Accumulation, ambiguous rewritten identity and no exit | Fixed question/ladder/due date, identity matching and due-date freezing |\n\n## Not yet designed\n\nHuman-review handling; upstream acquisition/classification and return notifications; category prompt wording; library open questions, particularly analyst-level thesis granularity; final verdict and pull mode beyond the decided freezing behaviour. A grouping requirement is not evidence that its implementation has passed acceptance.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D10/D17: classified stream and dispatch as the sole entry. D12–D14: no internal clock; separate stream and pull mode.\n- D16/D18/D19: human review, universe-owned scope and first module freezes.\n- D25–D28: one extraction module, shared analyst role, central library, sourced whole-item reading and report ingestion.\n- D29/D30: historical final-verdict and disagreement design, revised by D38/D54.\n- D31/D32: sequence order, one queue per thesis, whole table releases and no separate filing XBRL ingestion.\n- D33–D37: read-only extraction, SEC publication day, extraction review, glossary and first-version freezes.\n- D38–D42: rebuilt thesis structure, library intake/history, matching labels, aligned module boundaries and binary ladders.\n- D44/D45: initial model-owned matching; separate proposals from decisions; similar questions remain distinct. D155 later bounds code-owned identity in the POC.\n- D46/D49: one state, verifiable ladders, normal ingestion of decisive information, publication-relative years and top-N search.\n- D47/D48: consistent terms and complete comparison paths; library freeze.\n- D51: changed-input rejudgement, explicit conclusion change, dependencies chosen in matching, seven intake steps, no consensus calculation.\n- D52/D53: plain terms and full context review without added rules.\n- D54–D57: due-date freezing without a separate result or wait, manual universe maintenance and one open/closed switch.\n- D58–D60: clarify directions, waiting, due-date scope, manual operations, subject identification and personal memos; freeze the foundation except final verdict/pull mode.\n- D149: extraction-owned research-group association. D155: current identity and reference-list changes.\n- D163: English for maintained product text and documentation; historical evidence retains its original text.\n",
 "docs/prd/01-universe.md": "# Research universe\n\n> Status: first version frozen, October 18, 2026; specific themes remain a draft. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Research universe. Recorded decisions take precedence over this PRD.\n\n## Purpose\n\nDefine which subjects to research and which information categories to track for each. This is the sole owner of what the system processes.\n\nTariff research needs government notices; company research needs news and company announcements; Bitcoin research needs on-chain data. A second global category switch would eventually conflict with this list.\n\n## Scope\n\nList over 500 US-listed companies, cryptocurrencies, commodities and macro themes, each with tracked categories. Supply this scope to upstream. Also supply all themes to extraction block 3 so theme candidates can select one.\n\nThe universe does not filter items inside dispatch, choose reading methods or model settings, or automatically add subjects. Extraction owns reading methods; Config owns model settings. Unresolved identifiers and proposed themes go to human review; resolvable assets merely outside the universe do not.\n\nD155's POC entries include research references without changing this information scope. See the [improvement plan](../poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md) for their purpose and identity limits.\n\n## Four subject kinds\n\n<!-- generated: kinds -->\n| Kind | Identifiers | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Company | Ticker, CIK or company name | Over 500 US-listed companies: Apple, Amazon, Meta… |\n| Cryptocurrency | Cryptocurrency symbol | Bitcoin, Ethereum… |\n| Commodity | Commodity symbol | Gold, crude oil, copper… |\n| Theme | Theme ID | US inflation, US–China tariffs, Russia–Ukraine war… |\n<!-- /generated: kinds -->\n\n## Default tracked categories\n\n<!-- generated: defaults -->\n| Kind | Tracked information | Category IDs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Company | Company publications (earnings, periodic reports, calls, presentations and other releases), related legal and capital-markets documents regardless of filer, news, opinion and analysis, interviews, official actions, prices, holdings, calendars and third-party measurements | T1–T10, T12–T15 |\n| Cryptocurrency | News, opinion and analysis, official actions (enforcement, ETF approvals), prices, flows and holdings, unlock calendars and on-chain data | T7, T8, T10, T12–T15 |\n| Commodity | News, opinion and analysis, official actions (OPEC decisions, sanctions), statistics (EIA inventories), prices, holdings (CFTC), calendars, shipping and satellite data | T7, T8, T10–T15 |\n| Theme | No default; define categories for each theme | — |\n<!-- /generated: defaults -->\n\n## Draft theme library: 12 groups\n\n<!-- generated: themes -->\n| Group | Theme |\n|---|---|\n| Money and rates | Fed policy rate · US Treasury yield curve · ECB, BOJ and PBOC policy |\n| Inflation | US inflation · Wage growth · Energy price transmission |\n| Growth and employment | US recession risk · Labour market · Consumer spending · Chinese economy · European economy |\n| Fiscal policy | US deficits and debt issuance · Debt ceiling and government shutdowns · Tax policy |\n| Trade and industrial policy | US–China tariffs · Chip export controls · Industrial subsidies (CHIPS Act, IRA) · Supply-chain shifts |\n| Geopolitics and war | Russia–Ukraine war · Middle East conflict · Taiwan Strait · Sanctions |\n| Politics and elections | US elections · Policy direction |\n| Regulation and law | US/EU antitrust · Data and privacy · AI regulation · Financial regulation |\n| Energy and commodities | Crude oil supply (OPEC) · Natural gas · Industrial metals |\n| Exchange rates and capital flows | US dollar strength · Renminbi · Yen |\n| Industry cycles | AI capital expenditure · Semiconductor cycle · Cloud demand |\n| Market risk appetite | Credit spreads · Volatility · Liquidity |\n<!-- /generated: themes -->\n\n## Theme tracking examples\n\n<!-- generated: examples -->\n| Theme | Tracked information | Category IDs |\n|---|---|---|\n| US inflation | CPI, PCE and BLS releases, ISM price components and University of Michigan inflation expectations (macro statistics); related news; economist forecasts | T11, T7, T8 |\n| US–China tariffs | USTR and Commerce Department announcements (official actions); related news and commentary; company reports, calls and announcements discussing tariffs | T10, T7, T8, T1–T6 |\n<!-- /generated: examples -->\n\n## Rules\n\n<!-- generated: rules -->\n1. Manage and version the research universe separately from Config. The theme library is part of it, not a separate copy.\n2. The universe defines which subjects to research and which categories to track for each. It is the sole owner of what the system processes.\n3. Companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities each have default categories. Every theme explicitly specifies at least one category. Categories must come from the set of 16. A universe version is invalid if a subject cannot be resolved in reference data or the theme library.\n4. Upstream supplies only subjects and categories covered by the universe. Test code follows the same scope. Every supplied item is processed.\n5. Adding a subject or changing tracked categories creates a new version. Upstream backfills relevant historical items under the new universe.\n6. Rates, exchange rates and indices belong to themes, such as the US Treasury yield curve or US dollar strength, rather than separate subject kinds.\n7. Do not add subjects automatically. Unresolved identifiers and themes outside the universe go to the human review pool. Resolvable companies, cryptocurrencies or commodities outside the universe, such as Samsung, only receive a processing-log entry.\n8. Macro and geopolitical themes connect to companies through thesis dependencies. A theme thesis describes an outcome without calling it good or bad. An asset thesis records which theme theses it reads and which end of each is bullish for it. A changed conclusion triggers the library’s rejudgement rules for dependent theses.\n<!-- /generated: rules -->\n\n## Examples\n\n- An Apple company row uses company defaults: upstream sends earnings, 10-Ks, calls, news, prices and other tracked categories.\n- A US–China tariff theme can track official actions, news, opinion/analysis and company disclosures T1–T6: upstream sends USTR announcements, related reporting and commentary, and company reports, calls and releases mentioning tariffs.\n- Adding a company creates a new universe version; upstream backfills its relevant historical information.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Every version lists each subject's kind, name and tracked categories.\n- An invalid category, a theme with no categories, or an unresolved subject invalidates the version.\n- Any change creates a new version and preserves the previous one.\n- Dispatch contains no subject-based routing decision.\n- Nothing outside the universe is added automatically. Unresolved identifiers and new themes enter human review; resolvable out-of-universe companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities do not.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Specific themes in the draft library.\n- Upstream delivery and where its universe version is recorded.\n- Storage location and maintenance ownership.\n- Reference data used to validate subjects belongs to upstream; coordinate that contract with upstream.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D6: separate management and versioning. D11: four subject kinds; themes are part of the universe.\n- D16: unresolved identifiers go to human review. D18: the universe alone selects subjects/categories and supplies defaults.\n- D19, D37: first-version freeze; changes need a decision.\n- D24/D28: the earlier exclusion of financial reports was reversed; company defaults include T1, T2 and T4.\n- D26/D41/D42: macro effects reach companies through dependencies; use company as the subject kind.\n- D45/D46: the model selects dependency direction and code prevents cycles.\n- D47: distinguish unresolved identifiers and new themes from resolvable out-of-universe assets.\n- D51/D58/D60: dependent open theses follow the library's rejudgement rule; a changed conclusion is broader than a changed rung.\n- D59: all themes appear in extraction block 3.\n",
 "docs/prd/02-classification.md": "# Classification (upstream)\n\n> Status: first version frozen, October 18, 2026. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Classification. Recorded decisions take precedence over this PRD.\n\n## Purpose\n\nUpstream labels each item with a classification result, text/table form and related subjects. Most results are one of 16 categories; others are duplicate, not source material or invalid. Upstream follows this specification. Dispatch checks completeness and category validity without classifying again.\n\nThe old system defined categories in nine modules and twelve collections, which disagreed. This design owns one definition; consumers read the category and its attributes.\n\n## Scope\n\nProduce exactly one result, identify related subjects and specify input fields. Do not use a model, decide research scope (the universe owns that), or read incidentally mentioned companies from the body (extraction owns that).\n\n## The 16 categories\n\n<!-- generated: tags -->\n| ID | Category | Form | Family | Decision rule | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 | Earnings releases | Text and tables | Company publications and filings | Text: exhibits named by 8-K Item 2.02, fiscal-results exhibits in 6-K, exchange earnings announcements and sources labelled as earnings. Tables: datasets registered as earnings figures. | Apple quarterly earnings release, Tesla update deck, guidance history |\n| T2 | Periodic reports | Text and tables | Company publications and filings | Text: 10-K, 10-Q, 20-F, 40-F and ARS, including /A amendments; exchange annual, interim and quarterly reports; sources labelled as periodic reports. Tables: standardised financial statements. Do not separately ingest XBRL facts already in the filing. | Meta 10-K, 10-K/A, standardised income statement |\n| T3 | Legal and capital-markets documents | Text | Company publications and filings | Proxy, financing, M&A and tender-offer filings regardless of filer; exhibits numbered 2, 3, 4 or 10; exchange circulars; sources labelled as legal documents. | DEF 14A, activist DEFC14A, 424B5, merger agreement, credit agreement |\n| T4 | Company-call transcripts | Text | Company publications and filings | Transcripts of company-hosted calls or company presentations at investor conferences; merger calls in 425 filings. | MSFT earnings call, NVDA fireside chat at a broker conference |\n| T5 | Presentations | Text | Company publications and filings | Company slides: presentations under 8-K Item 7.01 or in 425 filings, and IR-site slides that are not earnings releases. | Investor-day deck, IR-site presentation not labelled as earnings |\n| T6 | Other company publications | Text and tables | Company publications and filings | Text: remaining company files, including 8-K bodies and other exhibits, remaining 6-K content, SEC response letters, late-filing notices, press releases and official posts. Tables: IR supplements and monthly operating data. | Executive-change 8-K, quarterly delivery announcement, IR KPI table |\n| T7 | News | Text | Media reporting | Material authored by media, with no registered publication series and no opinion or analysis section. A missing section defaults to news. | Reuters report, CNBC CEO interview, DigiTimes supply-chain report |\n| T8 | Opinion and analysis | Text and tables | Opinion and dialogue | Text: registered forecast, outlook or research series regardless of author; media opinion/analysis sections; analyst-authored text other than dialogue records; all individual-authored text; investor letters to boards in filings. Tables: forecasts, ratings, price targets, consensus and investor-sentiment surveys. | Sell-side initiation, short report, IMF World Economic Outlook, Reddit post, FY2027 EPS consensus, personal earnings model |\n| T9 | Interview records | Text | Opinion and dialogue | Analyst-authored transcripts or meeting notes. | Expert-network interview, CFO meeting notes |\n| T10 | Official actions and records | Text and tables | Official actions and macro statistics | Text: official series registered as actions or records; official authors without a registered series; SEC comment letters and court dockets. Tables: government-published records. | European Commission decision, FTC complaint, SEC comment letter, defence contract award, patent grant |\n| T11 | Macro policy and statistics | Text and tables | Official actions and macro statistics | Text: registered monetary-policy or statistical series, whether public or private. Tables: macro statistics and policy rates. | FOMC statement, Beige Book, CPI and payrolls, ISM PMI, federal funds rate |\n| T12 | Market prices and trading | Table | Market and measurement data | Datasets registered as prices and trading. | Daily prices, option implied volatility, 10-year Treasury yield |\n| T13 | Holdings and flows | Table | Market and measurement data | Datasets registered as holdings and flows; table versions of ownership filings. | Institutional holding changes, insider sales |\n| T14 | Calendar | Table | Market and measurement data | Datasets registered as scheduled dates. | Next earnings date, FOMC meeting date |\n| T15 | Third-party measurements | Table | Market and measurement data | Datasets registered as measured activity. | App downloads, card-spending data |\n| T16 | Reference data | Table | Market and measurement data | Datasets registered as identities and classifications. | Ticker-to-CIK mapping, GICS classification, S&P 500 weights |\n<!-- /generated: tags -->\n\n### Category definitions\n\n<!-- generated: definitions -->\n- **T1 Earnings releases**: Earnings releases for a quarter, half-year or full year. Text includes exhibits named by 8-K Item 2.02 (earnings releases, shareholder letters, CFO commentary, update decks), explicitly identified fiscal-results exhibits in 6-K, exchange earnings announcements and sources labelled as earnings. Tables include release-day results and guidance, including guidance history. Monthly operating data belongs to T6. Merge republications of the same release with the original.\n- **T2 Periodic reports**: Text: 10-K, 10-Q, 20-F, 40-F and ARS, including amendments that name the replaced version; exchange annual, interim and quarterly reports; sources labelled as periodic reports. Tables: vendor-standardised statements and segment data, identifying the source report. Do not separately supply XBRL facts already in those reports. Annual versus quarterly is an attribute.\n- **T3 Legal and capital-markets documents**: Proxy materials, including both sides of a proxy contest; financing, M&A and tender-offer filings and amendments, including acquirer and private-equity filings, regardless of filer. Include SEC exhibit categories 2, 3, 4 and 10 (acquisition agreements, charters, security/debt instruments and material contracts), exchange circulars and sources labelled as legal documents. Read terms and each party's statements; attribute statements to the submitting party.\n- **T4 Company-call transcripts**: Company-hosted earnings calls, investor days and company podcasts; company presentations at investor conferences; merger calls in 425 filings. Media-hosted events belong to T7. Venue and transcript provider are attributes.\n- **T5 Presentations**: Company slide decks and PDF presentations under 8-K Item 7.01 or in 425 filings, and IR-site decks that are not earnings releases. Sources labelled as earnings belong to T1.\n- **T6 Other company publications**: Text not covered above: 8-K bodies, exhibits not named in the earnings item, other items, remaining 6-K content, SEC response letters and late-filing notices; press releases, operating updates, ESG reports, newsrooms, blogs, IR/product pages and official company social posts. Tables: company-published supplementary information and monthly operating data, such as IR Excel or CSV files.\n- **T7 News**: Media publications other than opinion and analysis: news, interviews, podcast transcripts, media-hosted events such as WSJ Tech Live, and media-account posts. General, financial and trade media all qualify. A missing section defaults to news. Opinion and analysis sections belong to T8. Registered media series follow their registered category, such as Reuters economist surveys in T8.\n- **T8 Opinion and analysis**: Attributed opinions and analysis regardless of author, length or platform. Text includes sell-side and independent research, rating agencies, industry research, think tanks and academia; official and international-organisation forecasts, outlooks and research such as the IMF World Economic Outlook, CBO Budget Outlook and central-bank working papers; fund letters, short reports and investor letters to boards in filings; media opinion; personal posts and essays; and the user's investment memos. Tables include analyst forecasts and revisions, ratings, targets, consensus, credit/ESG ratings, economist and official forecasts including GDPNow nowcasts, investor-sentiment surveys, industry estimates and forecast upload templates. Opinions and estimates remain attributed statements, not completed facts. The user's own opinions become candidate theses, not evidence.\n- **T9 Interview records**: Dialogue recorded by analysts: expert-network interviews, management meetings arranged by the user and channel checks. The form is a transcript or notes. Attribute statements to the original speaker; the recorder is an attribute. Company-hosted events and company presentations at investor conferences belong to T4.\n- **T10 Official actions and records**: Official material not classified elsewhere. Text includes regulatory, judicial, legislative, administrative and exchange documents and proceedings: rulings, approvals, recalls, regulations, sanctions, export controls, tariffs, contract awards, hearing/trial transcripts, SEC comment letters and court dockets. Tables include government records of contracts, lobbying, political donations, patents, litigation, recalls and customs bills of lading. Official forecasts, outlooks and research belong to T8; statistics belong to T11. Officials' personal accounts count as official during their tenure, such as a president announcing tariffs. Attribute quoted statements to their original speaker or submitting party.\n- **T11 Macro policy and statistics**: Text registered as monetary policy or statistics, regardless of publisher: central-bank statements, minutes, speeches, dot plots and Beige Books; official CPI/jobs releases and private ISM PMI, ADP employment and Michigan sentiment releases. Tables: economy-wide or industry-wide statistics and policy rates, public or private, each with a publication date; retain earlier revisions. Include composite measures we cannot calculate because weights are unavailable, such as LEI and financial-conditions indices. Market interest and exchange rates belong to T12; measurements of specific companies, products, locations, chains and protocols belong to T15.\n- **T12 Market prices and trading**: Prices and trading in stocks, ETFs, option chains, futures, interest rates, FX, commodities, cryptocurrencies, credit spreads, CDS and prediction markets; corporate actions such as dividends and splits; share capital.\n- **T13 Holdings and flows**: Market-participant holdings and flows: table versions of Form 4, 13D/G, 13F and 144; short interest, borrow fees and utilisation; ETF/fund flows; CFTC positioning; cryptocurrency whale and smart-money holdings.\n- **T14 Calendar**: Scheduled dates for earnings, investor days, launches, macro releases, central-bank meetings, court/regulatory deadlines, index rebalancing, lock-up and token unlocks, and option expiries.\n- **T15 Third-party measurements**: Vendor-measured activity for specific companies, products, locations, chains and protocols: card spending, app usage, web/search/foot traffic, satellite and shipping data, hiring, scraped prices, reviews and on-chain usage/revenue. These are measurements, not estimates. Industry estimates and forecasts belong to T8; economy-wide or industry-wide statistics belong to T11.\n- **T16 Reference data**: Company and asset identities, industry classifications, corporate structure, supply-chain relationships, index constituents and weights. No subject tracks this category by default. Upstream uses identity references to resolve subjects.\n<!-- /generated: definitions -->\n\n## Decision order\n\n<!-- generated: routing -->\n1. First validate: missing required metadata, malformed subject identifiers, unreadable text or the system's own output → invalid. Previously received identical content without an explicit replaced version → merge with the original.\n2. Table data: register each dataset's category once at onboarding rather than judging every record. Split registration by field when an interface returns multiple kinds of content. All later records use that category. Unregistered datasets are invalid and go to human review.\n3. Text filings (SEC, exchange, court): use the filing-system code table regardless of filer. Unknown codes are invalid and go to human review.\n4. Other text with a registered publication series: use the series table (T8, T10 or T11), regardless of author type. An unregistered series name goes to human review while classification continues normally.\n5. Remaining text: resolve author type from the author table valid on the publication day, falling back to the channel default; then use section or genre. Official authors without a registered series → T10. Unresolved author type → invalid and human review.\n<!-- /generated: routing -->\n\n## The four lookup tables\n\n<!-- generated: lookup -->\n| Table | Lookup input and result | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dataset table | Dataset → category or not source material | Daily prices → T12; financial statements → T2; ISM PMI and LEI → T11; GDPNow → T8; technical indicators and fear/greed index → not source material |\n| Filing-code table, one per filing system | Filing code → category; 8-K, 6-K, 425 and ownership filings require additional rules | 10-K → T2; DEF 14A → T3; 8-K → inspect items and exhibits |\n| Publication-series table | (author, series) → T8, T10 or T11. Any author can register a series; T10 is official only. | FOMC statements, Beige Book, ISM PMI → T11; IMF World Economic Outlook, CBO Budget Outlook → T8; OFAC sanctions announcements → T10 |\n| Author table | Author → company, media, analyst, official or individual, with a validity period. Fall back to the channel default when unresolved. | Reuters → media; Morgan Stanley → analyst; IMF → official; president's personal account during tenure → official |\n\n- Keep all four tables in one JSON file read by production and tests. Changing a table creates a new classification version.\n- The first three tables return a result directly: category, not source material, or further rules such as 8-K. No intermediate name. The author table returns author type, not category: Reuters news is T7 and opinion is T8; section, genre or series still matters.\n- Dataset and series registration follow the same principles: public/private statistics → T11; central-bank monetary-policy/economic statements, minutes, speeches, dot plots and Beige Books → T11; central-bank research and working papers → T8; forecasts, outlooks and research → T8; actions and records → T10, official only. For measured activity datasets, economy/industry-wide → T11; specific company, product, location, chain or protocol → T15.\n- Validate table values at load: dataset → one of 16 categories or not source material; filing code → category or rule; series → T8, T10 or T11, with T10 official only; author → one of five author types. Invalid values prevent startup.\n- Do not guess missing datasets, filing codes or series; send them to human review for registration. Missing authors use channel defaults first and reach human review only if still unresolved.\n- Identifier-to-subject mappings, such as AAPL → Apple, are not classification tables. They resolve subjects and validate the universe, not categories. Upstream owns this mapping; details are deferred.\n<!-- /generated: lookup -->\n\n## Related subjects\n\n<!-- generated: objects -->\n1. Four subject kinds, matching the research universe: company, cryptocurrency, commodity and theme. An item may concern several subjects or none.\n2. Use only channel-supplied identifiers and their stated reasons, plus the author when it is a company: the issuing company for a release or the company speaking at its meeting. Record why each subject is attached. An empty or unsupported identifier kind or reason invalidates the whole item.\n3. Keep identifier kinds separate because equal strings can mean different subjects: companies use CIK, ticker or company name; cryptocurrencies and commodities use their own symbols; themes use theme IDs.\n4. Themes have only two origins: upstream delivery for that theme or explicit identification at upload. Do not infer them from the body text.\n5. Read identifiers, not body text or URLs. A filing agent's identifier can appear in a URL; an old Amazon 8-K URL contains agent ID 1104659.\n6. Resolve company, cryptocurrency and commodity identifiers through reference data. Pad CIK to 10 digits; resolve tickers as of publication day (FB identified Meta before June 9, 2022); accept only registered company aliases. Resolve theme IDs through the theme library. Preserve unresolved identifiers as received, mark them unresolved and continue processing; fill them after registration.\n7. A non-company author (media, analyst, official or individual) is not itself a subject. Preserve both filer and filing subject with their roles: in a 13D, the activist is the filer and the held company is the subject.\n8. Do not label incidentally mentioned companies or themes here. Extraction associates them with specific theses and evidence.\n9. When merging a duplicate, union both items' subjects and deduplicate them. Subjects do not affect category. Invalid items and non-source material receive no subjects.\n<!-- /generated: objects -->\n\n## Time rules\n\n<!-- generated: time -->\n1. Use the New York publication day. Time of day matters only for SEC filing-day calculation (rule 4). Processing order follows dispatch sequence, not publication time.\n2. Each item uses one date: publication day. Its described fiscal or observation period is an attribute. The stream has no concept of today's date; only pull mode acts on time.\n3. Do not separately record a channel delay of several days. No rule uses the computer clock; receipt time is for audit only.\n4. SEC publication day is the legal filing day. Acceptance before 5:30 p.m. uses that day; after 5:30 p.m., SEC publication is next business morning, so use the next business day.\n5. Merged duplicates use the earliest publication day: knowledge begins when the market could first see it.\n6. Corrections, updates, restatements, edits and category changes create new versions without overwriting old ones.\n7. An item without publication time cannot be placed on the timeline and is invalid.\n<!-- /generated: time -->\n\n## Classification rules\n\n<!-- generated: rules -->\n1. Category describes what the information is. Text/table form, length, platform and channel are attributes. A category can have both forms; form only determines whether classification uses the dataset table or text rules.\n2. Each item has exactly one result: a category, merge with the original, not source material, or invalid with a reason. Use fixed rules and lookup tables, not a model.\n3. The author is responsible for the content. Wires, republication sites, transcript providers, data vendors, social/contributor platforms, EDGAR filing agents and user uploads are delivery channels.\n4. Resolve author type from the author table, then channel defaults: news indices default to media, wires to company, and social/contributor/newsletter accounts to individual. Only an unresolved result after both is invalid. Registration is an attribute. International organisations such as IMF, World Bank, OECD, OPEC and WTO are official.\n5. Author-table entries have validity periods evaluated on publication day. Status follows tenure, not influence: an account is official during office and individual during campaigning, before inauguration or after leaving office.\n6. Registered publication series take precedence over author type: ISM PMI → T11; Reuters economist survey → T8; CBO Budget Outlook → T8; CBO monthly deficit data → T11.\n7. Transcript authorship follows the event host: company-hosted and investor-conference company presentations count as company; media-hosted as media; hearings/trials as official; expert-network events as expert network; user-arranged meetings as user.\n8. Resolve filings from a complete code table for each system (EDGAR, exchange, court), regardless of filer. /A amendments share their original category. Unknown filing codes are invalid and go to human review before registration and acceptance.\n9. Only exhibits explicitly named by 8-K Item 2.02 count as earnings releases. Classify each other exhibit separately.\n10. A filing body and each exhibit are separate items. Do not separately ingest XBRL facts already in the filing. Ownership filings (Form 4, 13D/G, 13F, 144) use their table form and belong to T13.\n11. Previously received identical content, matched by fingerprint or to the original, without an explicit replaced version, becomes another source of the original and retains its category. An explicit replacement, including a category-only correction, is a new version, not a duplicate.\n12. The same kind of opinion or analysis keeps its category regardless of author, length or platform. Broker, rating agency, columnist, investor, individual or user identity is an attribute. Attribute opinions and estimates to their author.\n13. Manual uploads must specify text genre or upload template; otherwise they are invalid. The system's own output is also invalid (classification step 1).\n14. A table release is one item, such as an entire 13F or statistical release, and enters as a batch. Split an interface's different kinds of content into separate items. Derived indicators we can calculate from existing information and file listings are not source material.\n15. Attribute quoted statements to their original speaker at the evidence level. The containing item's category does not change.\n16. Keep incidental differences as attributes: channel, platform, length, summary/full text, venue, annual/quarterly, holding status, executive status and registration. The host of an oral event does determine transcript authorship (rule 7).\n<!-- /generated: rules -->\n\n## Excluded material and invalid inputs\n\n<!-- generated: not-inputs -->\n- Vendor-derived indicators we can calculate from available information are not source material: valuation multiples, factor exposures, technical indicators and sentiment derived from price/volume such as fear/greed indices. If weights or underlying data are unavailable, classify by content: LEI and financial-conditions indices → T11; nowcasts such as GDPNow → T8.\n- File listings, such as EDGAR lists and news search results, locate information but are not source material themselves.\n- Audio, video, images and scans without readable text are invalid. Once a transcript or text layer exists, apply normal classification.\n- The system's own reports, exports and republished output are invalid, preventing self-citation.\n- Stock universes and watchlists belong to the research universe. The user's holdings, trades, P&L and investment permissions are not source material; their destination is deferred.\n<!-- /generated: not-inputs -->\n\n## Required input fields\n\nThese field names are the actual upstream contract.\n\n<!-- generated: fields -->\n| Group | Field | Meaning | Required | Used for classification |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `input_id` | The item's stable identifier | Required |  |\n| Identity | `content_sha256` | A content fingerprint identifies identical content. Without an explicit replaced version, accept identical content only once. | Required | ✓ |\n| Identity | `copy_of` | When the same content was received before and no replaced version is named, identify the original item and merge with it. | Identical content without an explicit replaced version | ✓ |\n| Identity | `supersedes` | An update names its previous version: updated news, 10-K/A, edited posts, data revisions or category corrections. Do not overwrite the previous version or merge the update as a duplicate. | When a previous version exists | ✓ |\n| Source | `channel` | Delivery channel: data interface, press-release wire, republication site, EDGAR, social/contributor platform or user upload | Required | ✓ |\n| Source | `source_uri` | Original location; use an accession number for SEC filings and a post ID for posts | Required |  |\n| Source | `publisher` | The author responsible for the content, not the channel forwarding it; use the account for social posts | Required | ✓ |\n| Source | `publisher_type` | Author type: company, media, analyst, official or individual. Resolve using the author table valid on the publication day, then the channel default. | Lookup table or channel | ✓ |\n| Source | `event_organizer` | Host of an oral event: company, investor conference, media, official body, expert network or user. This determines transcript authorship. | Transcripts | ✓ |\n| Source | `authors` | Named individual or real identity behind an account, used to attribute opinions | Optional |  |\n| Source | `derived_from` | The source item, such as the 10-K underlying standardised financial statements | When a source exists |  |\n| Source | `context` | Post context: reply target, quoted post, thread or forum | Optional |  |\n| Time | `period` | Period described by the content: fiscal period, observation window or event date | Periodic reports and data |  |\n| Time | `published_at` | When the author made it public; use the New York publication day. For duplicates, use the earliest day. | Required | ✓ |\n| Time | `filing_date` | SEC legal filing day: acceptance after 5:30 p.m. counts as the next business day, matching SEC publication. Use it as the SEC filing's publication day. | SEC filings | ✓ |\n| Time | `retrieved_at` | When we received it, kept for audit only and never used for decisions | Required |  |\n| Classification | `is_structured` | Table or text is a form, not a category. Tables contain fixed-field records (JSON, XBRL, XML, CSV, upload templates) and use the dataset table. Text follows classification rules. | Required | ✓ |\n| Classification | `has_readable_text` | Whether readable text exists; audio, video, images and scans without a text layer do not qualify | Required for text | ✓ |\n| Classification | `dataset` | Dataset, data interface or upload template; register its category once at onboarding and use it for every record | Tables | ✓ |\n| Classification | `filing_system` | Filing system: EDGAR, HKEX, court docket, etc.; each has its own code table | Filings | ✓ |\n| Classification | `filing_form` | Filing code: 10-K, 10-K/A, 8-K, DEF 14A, 425, etc. | Filings | ✓ |\n| Classification | `filing_items` | 8-K items and the exhibits named by earnings Item 2.02 | 8-K | ✓ |\n| Classification | `exhibit` | Exhibit number and description: 99.1 earnings release, 4.1 indenture, 10.1 material contract, etc. | Exhibits | ✓ |\n| Classification | `document_format` | Genre: transcript, notes, memo, report, slides, article, letter or post. The channel identifies it; uploads must specify it. Length and platform are attributes. | Text | ✓ |\n| Classification | `channel_category` | Source section: media news, opinion, analysis or republished press release; IR/wire/data-room earnings, periodic report or legal document | Optional; use the default if absent | ✓ |\n| Classification | `series` | Publication series from any public or private author, such as FOMC statements, Beige Book, ISM PMI or World Economic Outlook. Applies only if registered; unregistered names go to human review. | Optional; use author rules if absent | ✓ |\n| Content | `media_type` | File format: web page, PDF, JSON, XML, CSV, Excel, audio, video or image | Required |  |\n| Content | `language` | Language | Required |  |\n| Subject | `object_identifiers` | Channel-supplied subject identifiers, each with kind (CIK, ticker, company name, cryptocurrency symbol, commodity symbol or theme ID), value and reason (filing subject, filer, data field, channel tag, upstream delivery for that subject or explicit upload). May be empty. | Optional | ✓ |\n\n**Classification returns**: One category, merge with the original, not source material, or invalid with a reason. Categorised items also include resolved subjects (ID, kind, reason) and unresolved identifiers preserved as received.\n<!-- /generated: fields -->\n\n## Boundary examples\n\n| Material | Result | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Apple 8-K exhibit 99.1 named by Item 2.02 | T1 earnings | Resolve the filing code first |\n| Amazon 8-K filed via agent; URL contains agent 1104659 | Subject is Amazon | Read identifiers, not URL |\n| Activist letter to a board in a 13D | T8; investor is filer, held company is subject | Retain both roles |\n| FOMC statement, dot plot, Beige Book | T11 | Central-bank policy/economic material |\n| Fed working paper | T8 | Central-bank research |\n| IMF World Economic Outlook, CBO Budget Outlook | T8 | Forecasts and outlooks |\n| CBO monthly deficit, Treasury monthly statement | T11 | Statistics |\n| ISM PMI text report | T11 | Registered series precedes author; private statistics count |\n| ADP employment, Michigan sentiment data | T11 | Economy-wide measurement |\n| Card spending for one brand | T15 | Specific-company measurement |\n| AAII investor-sentiment survey | T8 | Aggregated opinions |\n| Fear/greed index | Not source material | Can be calculated from prices |\n| OPEC output-cut decision / monthly report | T10 / T8 | Action / outlook |\n| Reuters economist survey / report about IMF outlook | T8 / T7 | Registered series / ordinary news |\n| President's tariff post during tenure / campaign post | T10 / T8 | Author status follows tenure |\n| 2021 news tagged FB / tagged META | Meta / unresolved | Resolve ticker on publication day |\n| Apple news incidentally mentioning Samsung | Label Apple only | Extraction handles body mentions |\n| Amazon 10-Q accepted July 30, 2026 at 6:11 p.m. | Publication day July 31, 2026 | After 5:30 p.m., SEC publishes next business day |\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The classification simulation must pass its recorded populations: 412 real items, 161 hand-written cases and 24 company-subject cases. Script category IDs/names must match the page.\n- Every item receives exactly one result without a model.\n- All lookup values are valid; one invalid value prevents using the table version.\n- Every boundary example must become a test. The current simulation covers company subjects only; added macro rules and cryptocurrency, commodity and theme resolution remain known coverage gaps.\n- Resolve tickers on the New York publication day. The current simulator uses UTC dates; this known mismatch remains for implementation work.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Upstream acquisition and labelling, reference data and identifier-to-subject mapping.\n- Where the user's holdings, trades, P&L and investment permissions belong.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D1/D11: subjects and four subject kinds. D8: four tables in one JSON file with direct results.\n- D9: New York publication day. D15: macro rules, series precedence, private statistics and tenure-based author status.\n- D16: unresolved items go to human review. D19/D37: first-version freeze and decision requirement for changes.\n- D32: one table release per item; no separate filing XBRL ingestion. D34: SEC legal filing day is its publication day.\n- D56: the stream has no concept of today; only pull mode acts on time (D14/D54).\n",
 "docs/prd/03-config.md": "# Config\n\n> Status: first version frozen, October 18, 2026. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Config. Recorded decisions take precedence over this PRD.\n\n## Purpose\n\nConfig contains settings a person must choose: extraction model settings per category, separate matching and rejudgement settings, final-verdict settings, and the thesis search result count. Extraction owns category content and prompt assembly.\n\nThe old system had five overriding configuration layers and several presets. Multiple switches could control one feature, so a file did not reveal actual behaviour. Here the universe owns research scope, classification supplies item metadata, and code owns ordering and failure handling. Config retains only deliberate human choices.\n\n## Recorded settings\n\n<!-- generated: schema -->\n| Item | Meaning | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Version | Every settings change creates a new version. | Created when settings change |\n| Checksum | Calculated from the complete configuration and recorded with every model call, including extraction, matching, rejudgement and final verdicts, so the exact version can be traced. | Calculated by code |\n| Model settings and search result count | Extraction has settings per category: model, prompt version, reasoning effort and temperature (lower temperature makes answers more stable). The thesis library has separate matching and rejudgement settings; final verdicts have their own. Config also sets the search result count, initially 10. Prompt content belongs to the extraction, thesis library and final-verdict modules. A settings version is immutable; a change creates a new one. | Chosen by a person |\n<!-- /generated: schema -->\n\n## Rules\n\n<!-- generated: rules -->\n1. Config owns human-selected model settings and the thesis search result count. Upstream classification supplies category, publication day and subjects. Code owns processing order and failure handling. The research universe owns subjects and tracked categories.\n2. Config does not set spending or concurrency limits.\n3. Any change creates a new version. Preserve old versions without modifying them.\n4. Missing required values or references to nonexistent model settings invalidate the entire version. It cannot process any item.\n<!-- /generated: rules -->\n\n## Example\n\nChanging the news prompt creates a new configuration version. Subsequent news extraction uses it; other categories retain their settings. Earlier processing records still identify their original version.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Every extraction, matching, rejudgement and final-verdict call records Config version and checksum.\n- Missing required values or nonexistent model settings invalidate the entire version.\n- Any change creates a new version without changing the old one.\n- Config does not select processed categories or contain spending/concurrency settings.\n\n## Open questions\n\n<!-- generated: open -->\n- None currently.\n<!-- /generated: open -->\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D2: human choices only. D3: no spending/concurrency limits in Config. D18: category selection belongs to the universe.\n- D19/D37: first-version freeze; changes need a decision.\n- D25: extraction settings per category; reading content belongs to extraction.\n- D27/D33: separate matching settings. D29: final-verdict settings.\n- D41: separate model-call settings and temperature. D45: proposed duplicate checks are not approved and receive no settings yet.\n- D46: search result count N, initially 10. D47: Config is not limited to model settings.\n- D51: dependency selection occurs within matching, so the library has matching and rejudgement settings, not a separate dependency call.\n",
 "docs/prd/04-dispatch.md": "# Dispatch\n\n> Status: first version frozen, October 18, 2026. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Dispatch. Recorded decisions take precedence over this PRD.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe system's only entry point asks three questions in sequence to determine each item's destination.\n\nThe old system spread routing across daily steps, channel-specific text/table rules, filename-based model routing and category lists in nine modules. This design gives that decision one tree.\n\n## Scope\n\nCheck completeness, previous processing and classification. Give categorised items a sequence number and send them to extraction. Send issues to human review.\n\nDo not inspect subjects, filter categories already selected by the universe, choose research scope or perform date-based work.\n\n## Decision tree\n\n<!-- generated: tree -->\n- An item arrives → **Question: Is the item complete?** (Check author, publication day, classification result and upstream ID.)\n  - Incomplete → **Return**: This is an upstream problem; also place it in the human review pool.\n  - Complete → Check processing records, **Question: Has it been processed?** (Use the upstream ID. An updated version is a new item with its own ID.)\n    - Already processed → **Skip**: Do not pay again when upstream resends an item or a test reruns.\n    - Not processed → Read classification, **Question: What is the classification result?**\n      - Invalid → **Do not process**: Record the reason; unresolved lookup entries go to the human review pool.\n      - Not source material → **Do not process**: For example, derived indicators we can calculate ourselves, or a file listing.\n      - Duplicate item → **Merge with the original item**: Record another source for the original item; do not process it again.\n      - Assigned to a category → **Send to extraction**: Include its category and sequence number; extraction reads each category differently.\n<!-- /generated: tree -->\n\n## Notes\n\n<!-- generated: notes -->\n- Grey boxes stop processing. The black box sends the item to extraction.\n- Do not filter by subject here. Incoming items are already within the research universe, so dispatch does not decide which categories to process.\n- Every category goes to extraction with its category-specific reading method; no manual selection. Extraction defines what each category yields.\n- An unregistered publication series does not stop processing; also record it in the human review pool.\n- Assign sequence numbers in arrival order, without using the computer clock. All later steps use that order.\n- Processed means completed. Resume unfinished work from its recorded step rather than treating it as completed.\n<!-- /generated: notes -->\n\n## Examples\n\n| Input | Destination |\n|---|---|\n| Resent Reuters item with a completed upstream ID | Skip |\n| Updated version of that report, with a new ID | Process normally |\n| News without publication time | Return and record in human review |\n| Vendor-calculated P/E ratio | Do not process: not source material |\n| FOMC statement | Extract under T11 |\n| Reuters report | Extract under T7 |\n| Unregistered publication series | Classify using author rules, process normally and record in human review |\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Every item has exactly one destination.\n- A completed upstream ID is skipped on redelivery without another model call. An updated version has its own ID. Unfinished work resumes from its recorded step.\n- Every categorised item reaches extraction with its category and sequence number. Sequence follows arrival order, not the computer clock.\n- Routing does not read related subjects.\n- Incomplete items are returned and appear in human review.\n- Without an incoming item, dispatch does nothing.\n\n## Open question\n\nHow returned items notify upstream; decide with upstream.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D4/D18: no subject or category filtering here. D7: processing records determine prior completion.\n- D10/D17: dispatch is the system's sole entry. D16: issues go to human review.\n- D19/D37: first-version freeze; changes need a decision.\n- D25: one extraction module; dispatch no longer branches on text/table form.\n- D31: assign the sequence passed through every later step.\n",
 "docs/prd/05-extraction.md": "# Extraction\n\n> Status: foundation frozen, October 18, 2026; category prompt wording needs validation. D149 requires extraction-time grouping; D165 integrates it into the default pipeline (poc-v20), with a company's own financial lines carrying no group. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Extraction → Details. Recorded decisions take precedence over this PRD.\n> The step's standard (what is kept, how much it weighs, which questions open, the golden set and how a prompt change is measured) is the signed page `05-extraction-standard.html` (D243), which replaces `docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md` (D229). Where this PRD differs from it, the page governs.\n\n## Purpose\n\nRead each whole item with its category prompt as a senior hedge-fund analyst. Return evidence linked to shown or candidate theses, and candidates for important uncovered questions. Each candidate also carries structured group associations and any necessary new group definitions. Every output retains its source. Hand everything to the library, which owns the [grouping contract](06-views.md#research-groups-associate-during-extraction). Personal memos follow the prompt rule below.\n\n## Rationale\n\n- Extraction reads; the library compares and stores. Those responsibilities remain separate.\n- Read the complete file with embedded tables, or an entire table release, without separate text/table pipelines.\n- Categories require different reading: news for events and attribution, research for views and ratings, official decisions for actions and effective dates. They share one prompt structure.\n- A thesis is the analyst's judgement expressed as a question, ladder and due date. Evidence is an objective record of who said what in which item. Most items add evidence; fewer create new questions.\n- Evidence matters in relation to a question. Block 5 supplies existing subject theses and their dependencies, allowing a company statement about assured export licences to link directly to the licence theme.\n- Important uncovered matters, including another named company, a new material issue or a macro/policy event, become candidates with initiating evidence. The library later determines whether they are existing or new questions.\n- Sources make both theses and evidence traceable to the original text.\n\n## One item, step by step\n\nThe model performs step 2; code performs the others.\n\n<!-- generated: steps -->\n```\nAn item arrives with category and sequence number\n  ↓ 1 Build the prompt (Code): Use the category's content in the shared template. The library supplies block 5: open subject theses, their dependencies and existing groups needed for association.\n  ↓ 2 Read (Model): Read the whole item. Return evidence linked to block-5 theses or current candidates, and candidates for important uncovered questions with their initiating evidence. Personal memos follow their special rule. Decide each candidate's group association and return references under the library contract. Retain sources.\n  ↓ 3 Check sources and group references (Code): Check that the quote or cell exists in the item and that cited numbers appear there. Evidence must link to block-5 theses or current candidates. Reject source mismatches with reasons. Validate association references under the library's complete handoff contract.\n  ↓ 4 Hand off to the library (Code): Hand off evidence, candidates, associations and necessary group definitions with the sequence number. Matching decides identity; writing preserves membership.\n```\n<!-- /generated: steps -->\n\n## Two outputs: evidence and candidates\n\nOne reading returns evidence, candidates, group associations and necessary new group definitions. Evidence may link to shown theses or current candidates. Candidates are reserved for important uncovered questions, subject to the personal-memo rule. From extraction version 12 (D184) a candidate opens only for one of four admission reasons, stated in the answer: an annual quantity that carries the company's value, guided or not; one industry aggregate per calendar year; a stage of a policy process; a binding obligation a universe company takes on (due within 18 months, D185). The candidate also names the public material that settles it, the shown thesis the statement would otherwise attach to (or none) and what is lost if it only attached there. Code checks the shape of these fields; the model keeps the judgement of importance (D154/D155). A later estimate, another population or another wording of a shown aggregate is evidence to it. The library first resolves a candidate's destination, then compares its evidence within that thesis.\n\n<!-- generated: two -->\n|  | Evidence | Candidate thesis |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it is | A statement or number from the item: who said what or what happened | A complete question to track: subject, question, ladder, due date and research-group association |\n| How common | Present in almost every item | Few: important questions uncovered by block 5; also all judgements from the user's memo |\n| Links | One or more block-5 theses, or current candidates | Carries initiating evidence; candidates from the user's memo may omit it |\n| In the library | Compare against its thesis's existing evidence: repeat, replacement or new | Decide existing identity or a part of it, succession, new thesis or skip. Preserve associations with the final thesis; matching does not regroup. |\n| Example | The 8-K statement that H20 exports to China require a licence | What will the China export-licence regime for chips covered by the April 2025 rule be on April 15, 2026? |\n\n```\nRead a statement or number | Which thesis does it concern? (Judgements in the user's memo bypass this tree: all become candidates and may lack evidence)\n├─ A thesis in block 5 → Evidence: link to that thesis (Link only to the thesis directly discussed, not also to theses that depend on it)\n├─ Not in block 5, but important → Candidate thesis with this statement as evidence (Return its group association during extraction. The library later decides existing identity or new thesis. A thesis covers only up to its due date; events after that date belong here too.)\n└─ Neither → Do not extract (Background or boilerplate)\n```\n<!-- /generated: two -->\n\n## Prompt template\n\nAll categories use these five blocks. Classification owns the category's definition and reading method.\n\n<!-- generated: template -->\n| Part | Writes | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Role | You are a senior hedge-fund analyst. Read this item to test and form theses: link supporting or opposing statements and numbers as evidence to existing theses. For important questions not covered, return complete candidates with a question, ladder, due date, research-group association and initiating evidence. Judgements in the user's own memo become candidates, may lack evidence and are not themselves evidence. Each thesis concerns one thing. Its ladder has five rungs, or two for yes/no, with concrete, verifiable, non-overlapping and exhaustive outcomes, never vague “partial” outcomes. Set the due date when the issue can be resolved; for a number, use its publication date. Name the year. This year and next year are relative to the item's publication day; company numbers use its fiscal year. | Shared by all categories; thesis rules come directly from the library |\n| What this category is and how to read it | The category definition and reading method; for example, attribute opinions and estimates to their author rather than treating them as completed facts. | Owned by classification; not redefined here |\n| Subjects | The item's related subjects plus every theme in the research universe. Select an existing theme for a theme candidate when applicable; otherwise describe the new issue clearly. | Item metadata; themes from the research universe |\n| What to extract from this category | One row per category in the table below. | Extraction module |\n| Existing theses and research groups | Open theses for the item's subjects and their dependencies, with dependency links. Supply only question, focus and due date, not states or evidence. Also supply available research groups under the library's grouping contract. | Thesis library |\n<!-- /generated: template -->\n\n## Category-specific prompts\n\nT7 news illustrates how the five blocks fit together.\n\n<!-- generated: prompt -->\n```\nT7 news prompt (The same template, with news-specific blocks 2 and 4)\n├─ 1 Role → Senior hedge-fund analyst (Read to test and form theses. Link evidence to existing theses; for important uncovered questions, return complete candidates with question, ladder, due date and research-group association.)\n├─ 2 How to read this category → Media reporting (From classification: opinion and analysis belong to T8; registered publication series use their category; remaining media material belongs here.)\n├─ 3 Subjects → Subjects of this report (Filled by code, such as NVDA and AMD, plus all universe themes)\n├─ 4 What to extract → What happened, when and who was involved; attribution and original sources (Official confirmation is fact; unnamed-source claims are rumours)\n└─ 5 Existing theses and research groups → Open theses for the report's subjects and their dependencies (Filled by code with dependency links, questions, focus and due dates. For example, NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 and its chip-export-licence dependency. Also include available research groups.)\n```\n\n- Each category has its own prompt using the same template. Blocks 2 and 4 vary by category; block 1 is shared; code fills blocks 3 and 5 for the item.\n- One prompt per tracked category T1–T15. T16 is not tracked by default; write its prompt when needed.\n- For theme candidates, select from block 3 or describe a new issue if absent. For companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities, provide the relevant symbol or name. Code resolves reference data and accepts registered names only, as classification does.\n- The user's own memo (T8 authored by the user) yields candidate judgements, not evidence. These candidates may have no evidence. Once opened, their state is “not yet judged” until evidence first links to them.\n- Code defines and validates output fields for evidence, candidates and associations, necessary new groups and sources. Do not restate the shape in prompts.\n- Test each category's prompt on stored items before settling its wording. Config records each version; a change creates a new version.\n<!-- /generated: prompt -->\n\n## What each of the 16 categories yields: block 4\n\n<!-- generated: categories -->\n| Category | What to extract | Typical evidence tier |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 Earnings releases | Actual figures, guidance and management explanations | Actual figures are facts; guidance and explanations are party statements |\n| T2 Periodic reports | Actual and segment figures; risks and changes in risk factors and business descriptions | Figures are facts; described risks and changes are party statements |\n| T3 Legal and capital-markets documents | Transaction terms: price, consideration, closing conditions and timetable; parties' statements and demands | Terms are facts; statements are party statements |\n| T4 Company-call transcripts | Management answers to key questions, changes in wording and analysts' follow-up concerns | Answers are party statements; analysts' questions are third-party views |\n| T5 Presentations | Management's medium/long-term targets, capital-allocation framework and new-business scale | Party statement |\n| T6 Other company publications | Events such as executive changes, acquisitions, products and buybacks; published operating figures such as deliveries | Fact |\n| T7 News | What happened, when and who was involved; attribution and original sources | Official confirmation is fact; unnamed-source claims are rumours |\n| T8 Opinion and analysis | The author's opinions, arguments, forecasts, ratings and targets, and changes to them | Third-party view |\n| T9 Interview records | The speaker's account of orders, prices, share and inventory, and how they know | Third-party view |\n| T10 Official actions and records | The decision/action, effective date and affected parties; subsequent appeals and deadlines that may set the due date | Fact |\n| T11 Macro policy and statistics | Published figures and policy decisions versus the previous release; changes in official wording | Figures and decisions are facts; wording is a party statement |\n| T12 Market prices and trading | Changes in prices and trading | Fact |\n| T13 Holdings and flows | Who buys, who sells and at what scale | Fact |\n| T14 Calendar | Scheduled dates that can set due dates | Fact |\n| T15 Third-party measurements | Measured operating data as quantitative evidence | Fact; identify the third-party measurement |\n| T16 Reference data | Not tracked by default | — |\n\nRead every whole file, including tables. A table release is one item and is read as a whole batch. Do not separately ingest XBRL facts already in the filing. Whether to fetch exact settlement numbers is deferred to pull-mode design.\n<!-- /generated: categories -->\n\n## Example: two items\n\n<!-- generated: example -->\n```\nWhat two items yield (Illustration)\n├─ NVIDIA 8-K, April 15, 2025 → Empty library; no theses in block 5 | Two candidate theses (T6 other company publication: H20 exports to China require a licence; expected charges up to approximately $5.5 billion)\n│   ├─ Candidate: theme thesis → What will China export licensing for chips covered by the April 2025 rule, including H20 and MI308, look like on April 15, 2026? (Event thesis. Five rungs: full ban; licences required and none issued; licences issued with some conditions; all issued licences unconditional; no licence required. Due April 15, 2026. Quote: “…requires a license for export to China … of the Company’s H20 integrated circuits…”. NVIDIA discloses the licence requirement, classified as fact. The library opens thesis #1.)\n│   └─ Candidate: NVDA asset thesis → How will NVDA FY2026 China revenue compare with FY2025? (Numeric thesis. Five rungs: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Due on the FY2026 annual earnings date. Quote: “…up to approximately $5.5 billion of charges…”. This is a company expectation, classified as a party statement. The library opens #2, dependent on #1.)\n└─ NVIDIA statement, July 14, 2025 → Block 5 contains #2 and its dependency #1 | One evidence item, no candidate\n    └─ Evidence → Link to #1 (Party statement: NVIDIA says the US government assured it that H20 export licences to China will be granted (July 14, 2025))\n```\n\nEach evidence item records upstream ID, paragraph and exact quotation. Code checks that “$5.5 billion” appears in the quoted sentence. The first item sees an empty library, so important questions become candidates. The second sees the licence thesis and links NVIDIA's statement directly. The library compares candidates with searched theses and evidence within its thesis. Both candidates join the chip-licensing-and-China-business group; the evidence-only second item updates existing membership. Grouping does not replace #2's dependency on #1. The NVDA example uses the same process as equities, futures and cryptocurrencies.\n<!-- /generated: example -->\n\n## Source-reference fields\n\n<!-- generated: sources -->\n```\nA source reference\n├─ Which item → Upstream ID (Each version has its own ID; use it to retrieve the original from the team's library)\n├─ Category and day → Category and publication day\n├─ Where in the item → Section, paragraph or table cell (For table data, record dataset and record IDs)\n└─ Which statement → The original sentence or table cell (Code verifies the quote exists in the item and the cited number appears there)\n```\n\n- Every thesis, evidence item and information-triggered change retains sources. Each state change identifies its supporting evidence, plus dependency theses when their changes triggered rejudgement.\n- Extraction checks quote/cell presence, number presence and links to block-5 theses or current candidates. Reject output failing any check. The library refuses sources absent from processing records. Both record reasons.\n- Trace in both directions: thesis to all source items, and item to every thesis and evidence item it created or changed.\n- Do not store the whole source item; retrieve it from the team's library by upstream ID. Store only the quoted sentence or cell supporting the thesis or evidence.\n- Repeated statements of the same fact within one item, such as a number in text and a table, become one evidence item with multiple references.\n<!-- /generated: sources -->\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Give the model every whole item with its category prompt; record the settings version.\n- Every evidence link targets a shown thesis or current candidate. Reject missing or invalid targets with reasons.\n- Repeated statements of the same fact within one item, including text/table repetitions, produce one evidence item with multiple references.\n- Evidence contains a quotation, standalone summary, speaker, original source, tier and event time. A candidate contains subject, asset/theme kind, focus, question, ladder, due date, association, admission reason, settling material, the thesis it would otherwise attach to (or none), the loss if folded, and at least one evidence item; personal-memo candidates may lack evidence. A blank loss or settling material, an unshown thesis in the fold field, or a binding obligation due beyond 18 months is refused with its reason. Themes select from block 3 or clearly identify a new issue; assets use a symbol or registered name.\n- Every thesis and evidence item has a source. Reject absent quotes/cells or numbers not printed in the cited location, recording the reason.\n- Extraction does not mutate the library. Return evidence, candidates and associations together without a separate clustering call. Reference and semantic criteria are owned by the [grouping contract](06-views.md#research-groups-associate-during-extraction); similar names or equal counts do not prove correct grouping.\n- Retry from the failed step and replace only that attempt's own result.\n\n## Open questions\n\n<!-- generated: open -->\n- Validate each category prompt on stored items before settling it; record its version in Config.\n<!-- /generated: open -->\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D11: theses belong to subjects; extraction handles body mentions. D13: information-driven processing.\n- D25–D28: one extraction module, shared analyst role, category prompts, whole-item reading, sources and restored report ingestion.\n- D32/D33: whole table releases, no separate filing XBRL ingestion, read-only extraction with matching/writing in the library.\n- D35/D37: first-version review and freeze; changes require a decision.\n- D41/D42: candidates and block-5 context updated for the rebuilt library; separate evidence/candidate outputs with existing dependencies shown.\n- D45: only recorded decisions apply; no unapproved preset settlement source or theme-scope restriction.\n- D46: write the complete candidate while the full source is available.\n- D47: explicit dependency links; link a statement only to the question it directly concerns.\n- D51: omit unapproved restrictions on theme observations; evidence-link quality checks belong with matching labels; no consensus calculation.\n- D58: include due dates in block 5; events after an existing due date can form candidates.\n- D59/D60: subject identification and personal-memo candidates; no judgement before evidence first links; wording and diagram alignment without new rules.\n- D149: associations are required extraction output; the default implementation has not yet integrated them.\n- D155: current POC identity/reference changes are described in the improvement plan, not evidence of grouping acceptance.\n\n<a id=\"抽取时同时确定研究归属\"></a>\n\n## Determine associations during extraction\n\n<!-- generated: relations -->\n- D149 requirement: while reading the full text, decide which continuing business process each candidate belongs to, called a research group. Rubin shipments and SK factory deployment retain separate questions while sharing the Rubin rollout group. Group during extraction, not in the frontend or a later whole-library clustering pass.\n- Return evidence and candidates separately. Each candidate also carries structured associations; new groups have a name and scope. Mentioning a relationship only in prose is not a complete handoff. Extraction is read-only; the library's “Research groups: associate during extraction” section owns the full handoff and storage contract.\n- Grouping remains a target requirement, not a default-pipeline handoff. D155 fixed reference-list vetoes, period/scope rewriting and same-batch duplicates; that does not prove grouping works. See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for steps and measurements.\n<!-- /generated: relations -->\n",
 "docs/prd/06-views.md": "# Thesis library\n\n> Status: foundation frozen, October 20, 2026. D149 adds extraction-owned grouping; D165 puts it in the default pipeline. D169: the matching step runs on Jev by default (`poc-v23`); D168 states the Replace row's full definition and the succession answer it needs. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163). Only recorded decisions apply; open questions and two pull-mode proposals remain separate.\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Thesis library → Details. This PRD also owns Matching labels. Recorded decisions take precedence. Historical discussion: `docs/views-rebuild-log.md`.\n\n## Purpose\n\nA thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare evidence within its thesis to repeat, replace or add. Extra source references alone do not trigger rejudgement. Extraction determines group associations; the writer preserves them after resolving final thesis IDs under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction).\n\n## Rationale\n\n- Clear structures connect theses, ladders and evidence to source items.\n- A thesis is a question with a state, not an assertion. Opposing views move the same state's direction rather than creating opposite duplicate theses.\n- A fixed, verifiable ladder states in advance what would disprove the view, analogous to explicit analyst scenarios or predefined investment exit conditions. Do not change it after observing the outcome.\n- The model judges from all evidence still in force and dependency conclusions, at low temperature, rather than adjusting its previous score. Replacement follows statement date, so late old statements do not restore obsolete evidence.\n- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.\n- A thesis contains one period and one due date, generally 6–12 months; intermediate quarterly reports are evidence. A thesis on a binding obligation a universe company takes on (a guarantee, financing, an equity stake, a purchase commitment) may set its own check day up to 18 months after the item, because the signing can be checked long before what it finances is built (D185).\n- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.\n- Evidence needs the same search-then-compare discipline. Otherwise February's 12% capex guidance and April's 25% revision remain side by side, forcing every judgement to rediscover which is current.\n- Record repeat/replacement decisions once. Keep replaced evidence in history. A retelling alone should not require another model judgement to establish that nothing changed.\n- Use two rungs for yes/no instead of forcing five.\n- The model chooses dependencies like an analyst; code rejects cycles. Apparently mutual dependencies indicate poorly defined questions.\n- Rejudge when inputs change. A changed conclusion includes a new rung or materially changed supporting facts/numbers. A repeated Fed statement should not trigger hundreds of dependants; AMZN capex moving from 12% to 25% can trigger NVDA even within the same rung.\n- Start matching with a general-purpose model, retain records, obtain human labels and later train a faster smaller model.\n\n## Structure\n\n<!-- generated: structure -->\n```\nThesis library\n├─ Subject → Companies, cryptocurrencies, commodities and themes in the universe\n│   └─ Thesis → A question + a ladder + a due date (Immutable once written. Five rungs, or two for yes/no. When matching opens a thesis, only one may be open for the same subject and focus at a time.)\n│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model judges from facts. A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)\n│       ├─ Link → Evidence (Store evidence once with sources and tier. Its push belongs to the link. Replaced evidence remains in history and no longer counts.)\n│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)\n│       └─ Succession → The previous period's thesis (Successive periods of the same focus form a chain)\n├─ Research group → Organise theses by a continuing business process (Associate during extraction; full rules are in “Research groups: associate during extraction”)\n└─ Append-only records → Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labelling records (Append at every step, without editing or deleting. Read records up to a historical date to recover its judgement.)\n```\n<!-- /generated: structure -->\n\n## Two thesis kinds\n\n<!-- generated: kinds -->\n| Kind | Subject kind | Ladder rungs | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Asset thesis | Tradable subjects: companies, cryptocurrencies and commodities | Five rungs: strongly bullish, bullish, neutral, bearish, strongly bearish. Two for yes/no: bullish and bearish. These describe the business impact; set them at creation and keep them fixed. | How will NVDA FY2027 China revenue compare with FY2026? |\n| Theme thesis | Macro, policy and industry themes | Ordered outcomes from one end to the other: five rungs, or two for yes/no. Name outcomes without judging them good or bad. Dependent asset theses specify which outcome benefits them. | How will the Fed's target-rate upper bound after its last 2026 meeting compare with now? (Large cut … large hike) |\n\nThe same event can affect assets differently: rate cuts can be bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM net interest income. Theme theses therefore do not label outcomes good or bad.\n<!-- /generated: kinds -->\n\n## Ladder\n\n<!-- generated: ladder -->\n```\nTwo kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)\n├─ Asset thesis: numeric → NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 | Five rungs describing NVDA's business impact (Due on the FY2027 annual earnings date)\n│   ├─ Strongly bullish → Up at least 50%\n│   ├─ Bullish → Up at least 10% but under 50%\n│   ├─ Neutral → Up or down by less than 10%\n│   ├─ Bearish → Down at least 10% but under 80%\n│   └─ Strongly bearish → Down at least 80% (At most one fifth remains)\n└─ Theme thesis: event → China export licensing for chips covered by the April 2025 rule, including H20 and MI308, as of April 15, 2026 | Five ordered outcomes, without good/bad labels (Use the rules in force on that date)\n    ├─ Rung 1 → Full ban; licence applications no longer accepted\n    ├─ Rung 2 → Licences required; none issued\n    ├─ Rung 3 → Licences required and issued; some conditional\n    ├─ Rung 4 → Licences required and issued; all unconditional\n    └─ Rung 5 → Licences no longer required\n```\n\n1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed.\n2. Each rung is verifiable: a numeric interval or event stage. Rungs must be exhaustive and non-overlapping, so exactly one applies at expiry. For example: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Every boundary belongs to one rung. “Up a lot/a little” cannot be checked. Avoid vague partial outcomes; specify the partial state, such as conditional licences, as a distinct rung. State the comparison underlying neutral.\n3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.\n4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.\n5. Numeric theses name a year. Non-company quantities use calendar years: the publication year is this year and the following year is next year, each ending December 31. Company quantities use the fiscal year containing publication day and its successor. Never use the computer clock. For a given quantity, open at most one thesis for each of this year and next year; do not open later years. The due date is the quantity's publication date.\n6. Set the due date when the debate can be resolved, usually 6–12 months away. Intermediate quarterly reports are evidence, not separate theses.\n<!-- /generated: ladder -->\n\n## State\n\n<!-- generated: positions -->\n|  | Meaning | Decision maker | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges at low temperature from facts. Reconsider all evidence still in force, including each item's replaced predecessor to show the change, plus current dependency states and reasons and, for an asset thesis, the theme block. Do not increment or decrement the previous score. Cite evidence and compare with the conclusion before this round to state whether it changed. Do not rejudge if only source references were added and dependency conclusions did not change. | After the 8-K: strongly bearish |\n\n- Every judgement states its basis in a fixed response shape: evidence IDs such as e12 and e15, plus dependency thesis IDs such as #1 when their changes triggered rejudgement.\n- Code checks cited IDs against records: evidence must belong to this thesis and not be replaced; cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then human review after repeated failure. This checks references, not whether the judgement is correct, like verifying a cited page exists.\n- Each judgement also declares whether the conclusion changed and explains why, comparing with the state and reasons before this round, including during retries. That earlier conclusion is for comparison, not incremental scoring. A rung change or a material change in the supporting facts/numbers counts: AMZN capex guidance moving from 12% to 25% growth changes the conclusion even within one rung. Repetition does not. A changed conclusion triggers dependent theses in the same round. Code requires changed=true for a first judgement or rung change; otherwise refuse under the same correction rules.\n- For historical judgement, read the records saved on that date. Do not ask the model again; a new sample can differ.\n<!-- /generated: positions -->\n\n## Four evidence tiers\n\n<!-- generated: tiers -->\n| Tier | Example | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fact | Official actions, published figures and completed transactions | Can establish which rung holds and move the state |\n| Party statement | Company guidance, management statements and official speeches | Can move the state only |\n| Third-party view | Sell-side analysts, media analysis and experts | Can move the state only |\n| Rumour | Unnamed-source claims and unattributed statements | Can move the state only |\n\n- The model determines the size of the push and gives reasons. No tier has a fixed score.\n- Count independent original sources. Twenty outlets retelling one exclusive are twenty references to one evidence item, not twenty independent confirmations.\n- Tier follows who confirmed it. An unnamed-source report that Commerce is issuing licences is a rumour. An official or company confirmation is factual evidence replacing it. The rumour remains a rumour in history.\n- Store evidence once without an intrinsic direction. Each rejudgement writes its push on the evidence–thesis link. One statement can affect multiple theses differently.\n<!-- /generated: tiers -->\n\n## Dependencies: reading the arrows\n\n<!-- generated: dependencies -->\n```\nB depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)\n│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)\n│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)\n└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)\n    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)\n    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”\n```\n\n- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.\n- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.\n- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushes it; no edge is written for it. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.\n- Count a statement once. A statement about A is A's evidence, including when two candidates arise from one item. B learns it through A's conclusion. Only statements directly about B are B's evidence. If B does not depend on A, the statement may also directly evidence B. Choose dependencies only when B is created.\n- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, a dependency declaring a changed conclusion, or (for an asset thesis) a theme thesis declaring one. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Closed theses do not update. A memo-derived thesis is first judged when evidence first links to it; before that, it and its dependants see “not yet judged”.\n- The matching model chooses dependencies when opening a thesis, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction. Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.\n- Before adding an edge or following it to a successor, code checks for cycles. Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling. Apparently mutually dependent questions indicate poorly defined theses.\n<!-- /generated: dependencies -->\n\n## Succession: the same focus over successive periods\n\n<!-- generated: chain -->\n- Successive periods of the same focus form a chain, such as Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 rate → 2028 rate. The chain records period order. Dependency traversal follows the rules above.\n- One thesis has at most one successor: FY2026 cannot produce two FY2027 successors.\n- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.\n<!-- /generated: chain -->\n\n## Intake: search then compare\n\n<!-- generated: shape -->\n|  | Candidate thesis | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Comparison set | Top N search results from the entire thesis library | Top N evidence results within its thesis |\n| Decision | Existing identity or part, succession, new thesis or skip | Repeat, replace or new evidence |\n| Chain | Succession: Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 → 2028 | Replacement: AMZN capex guidance 12% growth → 25% |\n| What counts as current | Open theses on the chain; numeric theses may have one for this year and one for next year | Evidence not replaced; replacements also form a chain |\n| Search scope | Entire thesis library, open and closed | Only evidence within its thesis, not the entire library |\n<!-- /generated: shape -->\n\n## One item entering the library\n\nProcess one round per item in sequence order. Candidates arrive with associations and necessary group definitions, preserved under the grouping rules. The model performs steps 4 and 7; code performs the others.\n\n<!-- generated: intake -->\n```\nEvidence and candidates from extraction: one round per item\n  ↓ 1 Check sources (Code): Reject sources absent from processing records and record the reason\n  ↓ 2 Receive evidence (Code): Retries do not duplicate evidence. A revised input updates only its own source reference. A revised retelling does not alter the original evidence. If the revision is from the original source, compare its statements normally and replace changed statements.\n  ↓ 3 Search (Code): Use a search engine such as Elasticsearch; semantic versus keyword search remains open. Search all open and closed theses for each candidate and take top N, initially 10 in Config. Search evidence only within its linked thesis, also top N. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, even if also linked to existing theses. Empty lists still proceed to matching.\n  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The model does not read the original item. Show each existing thesis's subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state. In one call: (1) decide candidate identity or a narrower part, succession with unchanged ladder orientation but new thresholds, new thesis for distinct or merely similar questions, or skip overly broad questions, numbers beyond this/next year and the same period of a closed thesis; give reasons; (2) choose dependencies and directions among searched, block-5 and same-item new theses; (3) compare evidence linked only to existing theses. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, then searches all linked existing theses together and uses one further comparison call. Evidence linked only to new theses is new evidence.\n  ↓ 5 Validate (Code): Destination IDs must be in the shown lists or the relevant thesis's evidence. Merge only into open theses. New subjects must resolve within the universe using classification's identifier rules; otherwise skip. Dependencies must reference shown theses or an explicit awaited chain/year and remain acyclic. Return invalid answers for correction.\n  ↓ 6 Write to the library (Code): Resolve extraction's group associations to final theses and save them in this step. Each subject has its own writing queue; write theme theses before asset theses. Save new theses with their dependency edges. A repeat adds a source reference and any new thesis link. For replacement, order by when the statement was made: later statements supersede earlier ones. A late-arriving older statement is logged, not inserted. Carry the old evidence's other thesis links to its replacement. An original-source revision that omits a statement without replacing it marks it replaced. If compared data changes before writing, repeat matching only for the affected part.\n  ↓ 7 Run a rejudgement round (Model): Rejudge only open theses whose inputs changed: new/replaced/newly linked evidence, or a changed dependency conclusion, newly created awaited thesis or successor. Judge dependencies first, then theme theses before asset theses, and each thesis once per round. Extra source references alone do not trigger it. Memo-derived theses without evidence stay not yet judged. Each judgement declares whether its conclusion changed; changed conclusions propagate to dependants, and a theme's to every open asset thesis (D195). The round's queue is read from the ledger, so a resumed round recovers what an earlier commit queued.\n```\n<!-- /generated: intake -->\n\n## Step 4: candidate destinations\n\n<!-- generated: outcomes -->\n| Destination | When | Writes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Merge into existing | The candidate concerns the same focus or a part of it, such as H20 China revenue within China revenue. Merge only into open theses; closed theses can only be succeeded. | Compare its evidence within the target thesis and preserve grouping under this section's rules. Use the existing ladder and due date. |\n| Succession | The next period of the same focus: the next year for numbers, or continued tracking after an event thesis closed | Create a thesis and record succession |\n| New thesis | Distinct or merely similar: Blackwell export licences and H20 export licences are different questions | Create a thesis with its ladder and due date fixed from now on |\n| Skip | Too broad or mixed, bundling questions that can move oppositely, such as all US chip controls on China; numbers beyond this/next year; the same focus and period as a closed thesis; or an out-of-universe subject. Proposed themes go to human review; out-of-universe companies receive only a processing-log entry. | Do not create it. Evidence also linked to a specific valid thesis still counts; evidence linked only to the skipped candidate is not stored. |\n\n- The D155 POC uses code for fully determined duplicate identity and model comparison when scope or legacy identity needs judgement. A shared quantity (an aggregate opened under its admission reason) is one thesis per theme, measure and period: its population or wording is not identity, so code merges another wording into the open one and succeeds it for the next period (D197). Reference lists guide research without vetoing candidates. Same group does not mean same thesis. See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for the contract and measured scope.\n- Search finds plausible candidates; the model handles semantic comparison. H20 and Blackwell export licences can look similar but are separate questions. Bullish and bearish accounts of one question share its identity.\n- Search includes closed theses, allowing an FY2027 candidate to find closed FY2026 and be classified as succession.\n<!-- /generated: outcomes -->\n\n## Step 4: evidence destinations\n\n<!-- generated: evidence-outcomes -->\n| Destination | When | Writes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Repeat | The same evidence already exists under the thesis: a retelling, the same number in text and table, or the same speaker repeating the same statement | Add a source reference, not another evidence item. Existing links do not trigger rejudgement; a new thesis link changes that thesis's inputs and does. |\n| Replace | A later account of the same matter: a speaker revises guidance, forecast or event progress; an official confirms/denies a rumour; or the original source revises its statement | Order by statement date. Later statements replace earlier ones, which remain in history; rejudge. A late-arriving older statement is only logged and does not trigger rejudgement. |\n| New evidence | Anything else: a new event, another period's figure or another speaker's statement, including disagreement. Uncertainty also means new evidence. | Add it alongside existing evidence and rejudge |\n\n- First resolve the evidence's thesis destination from extraction or candidate matching. Search the top N evidence items still in force within that thesis and ask the model to compare. Unlike candidate-thesis search, evidence search never spans the whole library.\n- Store evidence once even when linked to several theses. Compare all those theses' evidence together. If any link is to a candidate, wait for that candidate's destination.\n- Carry other thesis links from replaced evidence to the replacement and rejudge open linked theses; otherwise they would retain only an obsolete statement.\n- Replacement applies to a statement or number, not the whole report. A revised sell-side revenue forecast replaces that forecast while other arguments remain in force.\n- Matching reads the evidence summary, speaker, original source and statement date, not the full original item. When uncertain, treat it as new evidence; both then remain available for judgement.\n<!-- /generated: evidence-outcomes -->\n\n## Example: three items\n\n<!-- generated: intake-example -->\n```\nThree items, starting with an empty library (Illustration)\n├─ NVIDIA 8-K, April 15, 2025 → Empty library; two candidates arrive | Empty comparison lists; open both as new theses\n│   ├─ #1 Theme thesis → China export licensing for this chip class: event (Evidence: H20 exports to China require a licence (fact))\n│   └─ #2 NVDA asset thesis → FY2026 China revenue: numeric (Evidence: expected charges up to approximately $5.5 billion (party statement); depends on #1, with easing bullish)\n├─ AMD 8-K, April 16, 2025 → AMD has no existing theses; two candidates arrive | Match\n│   ├─ Candidate: MI308 export licensing to China → Merge into #1 (Search finds #1. The model identifies the same question: the same chip class under the same rule. AMD's MI308 statement is new evidence. #1 is rejudged but declares its conclusion unchanged: an additional affected model does not change the key facts or rung. #2 is therefore not rejudged.)\n│   └─ Candidate: AMD 2025 China revenue → New thesis #3 (AMD has no open thesis; evidence is expected charges up to approximately $800 million (party statement))\n└─ Reuters, April 16, 2025, retelling NVIDIA's 8-K → Existing context: #2 and its dependency #1 | Match\n    └─ Evidence links to #1 and #2 → Both are repeats (The original source is the same NVIDIA 8-K and both statements already exist under #1 and #2. Add a source reference to each; neither thesis is rejudged.)\n```\n<!-- /generated: intake-example -->\n\n## Example: AMZN 2026 capex evidence updates\n\n<!-- generated: evidence-example -->\n| Item | Evidence comparison | Library result | Rejudgement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| February 5 call: capex guidance up 12% | New evidence; none previously under this thesis | Add g1 | Rejudge |\n| February 6 Reuters report retells the guidance | Repeat of g1 | Add a source reference to g1 | No rejudgement |\n| March 10 Morgan Stanley forecast: up 18% | New evidence from another speaker | Add m1 | Rejudge |\n| April 30 call: guidance revised to 25% growth | Replace g1 | g2 is in force; g1 is replaced | Rejudge, reading “12% → 25%” |\n| May 2 Morgan Stanley forecast revised to 27% | Replace m1 | m2 is in force; m1 is replaced | Rejudge |\n| Arrives May 20: Morgan Stanley's February forecast | Late older statement; Morgan Stanley already revised it in March (m1) and May (m2) | Do not insert this forecast. Log it as superseded and unused; m2 remains in force. | No rejudgement |\n| June 10 meeting reiterates “still around 25%” | Repeat of g2 | Add a source reference to g2, latest June 10 | No rejudgement |\n\nAfter six months the thesis may have dozens of evidence items. Rejudgement reads those still in force: company guidance of 25% growth (12% in February), Morgan Stanley's 27% forecast (18% in March), plus other speakers and events.\n<!-- /generated: evidence-example -->\n\n## Matching labels\n\nInitially a general-purpose model judges step 4. Retain every decision and have people label code-refused cases and periodic random samples: candidate destinations, dependencies and evidence decisions. When sufficient labels exist, train a faster smaller model to take over. Details remain deferred.\n\n<!-- generated: judge -->\n```\nFirst version: general-purpose model matching\n  ↓ 1 Large-model matching (Model): Start with a general-purpose model using Config's matching settings\n  ↓ 2 Record every comparison (Code): Record what the model saw, what it returned and what was ultimately stored\n  ↓ 3 Human labelling (Person): Label two sets: code-refused decisions from validation step 5 and periodic random samples. People supply the correct outcome.\n  ↓ 4 Train a smaller model (Code): Use human labels to train a faster, smaller model\n  ↓ 5 Smaller model takes over (Code): A smaller model eventually handles matching; the handover criterion is not yet decided\n```\n<!-- /generated: judge -->\n\n### Matching-record fields\n\n<!-- generated: trace -->\n| Part | Recorded content |\n|---|---|\n| Which item | Upstream ID and sequence number |\n| Received input | Preserve extraction's candidates, associations, new group definitions and evidence exactly. Separately record the matching-model input. |\n| Shown to the model | Ranked top N thesis matches per candidate; top N evidence matches within each thesis; extraction block-5 theses and same-item new theses available for dependency selection |\n| Decision maker | Large model, future smaller model or person |\n| Request and response | Actual request, raw response and Config version identifying model, prompt and temperature |\n| Decision | Each candidate's destination and reason; new-thesis dependencies and directions; evidence repeat/replacement/new decisions; validation results and refusal reasons |\n| Stored result | What was finally written to the library |\n<!-- /generated: trace -->\n\n## Open or closed\n\n<!-- generated: lifecycle -->\n```\nA thesis | Open or closed? (This is the only lifecycle switch)\n├─ Open → Rejudge when its inputs change, under the rejudgement rule (When matching opens theses, only one for the same focus at a time; numeric quantities may have one each for this year and next year)\n└─ Closed → No updates or rejudgement (Retain its closing state: the last rung if judged, otherwise not yet judged. Keep it searchable in the library.)\n```\n\n- Pull mode closes it when due. The current rung and reasons become its frozen final conclusion. To keep tracking, create a successor. Pull mode runs at day-end or scheduled intervals; the incoming stream does not manage time. Remaining pull-mode design is deferred.\n- People may manually close or reopen a thesis, for example after a delisting. Append a record naming the person and the sequence after which it applies. The matching rule of one open thesis per focus does not restrict manual reopening. People also maintain the research universe.\n<!-- /generated: lifecycle -->\n\n## Thesis fields\n\n<!-- generated: fields -->\n| Part | Writes | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Subject | The subject in the research universe | NVDA |\n| Kind | Asset thesis or theme thesis | Asset thesis |\n| Focus | Numeric: which quantity and year; event: which event | FY2027 China revenue |\n| Question | One sentence, immutable once written | How will NVDA FY2027 China revenue compare with FY2026? |\n| Ladder | Five rungs, or two for yes/no; each is a verifiable outcome, immutable once written | Up at least 50% … down at least 80% |\n| Due date | When the debate can be resolved; for numbers, their publication day | FY2027 annual earnings date |\n| State | Current rung; append each judgement with reasons and whether the conclusion changed. A memo-derived thesis is not yet judged until evidence first links to it. | Strongly bearish |\n| Dependency | Dependency targets and directions; for uncreated targets, the awaited chain and year | → China chip export licensing: easing is bullish |\n| Research-group association | Group memberships, stored under this section's grouping rules | China licensing and business for this chip class |\n| Succession | Predecessor, if any | Succeeds FY2026 |\n| Origin | The item that created it | NVIDIA FY2026 annual earnings release, February 25, 2026 (illustration) |\n| Open or closed | One switch: open or closed. Pull mode freezes and closes at expiry; people may close or reopen manually, such as after a delisting. Closed theses do not update. | Open |\n<!-- /generated: fields -->\n\n## Evidence fields\n\n<!-- generated: evidence -->\n| Part | Writes |\n|---|---|\n| Quotation | The original sentence or table cell |\n| Standalone summary | A sentence understandable without the original: who, what, how much and which period. Matching and rejudgement read it. |\n| Source | Upstream ID, category, publication day and location. Retellings, repeated numbers in text/tables and repeated statements add source references to the same evidence item. |\n| Speaker | The person or organisation making the statement |\n| Original source | For a retelling, identify who originally said it and on which day. Count independent sources and order replacements by that origin. |\n| Tier | Fact, party statement, third-party view or rumour |\n| Event time | When the event happened or takes effect; for a number, the period it describes |\n| Replaced | The replacing evidence, where a speaker revises a statement/number, an official confirms/denies a rumour, or the original source revises/deletes it. Deletion has no replacement evidence. Replaced evidence no longer counts but remains in history. |\n<!-- /generated: evidence -->\n\n## Ordering, concurrency and traceability\n\n<!-- generated: order -->\n- Only incoming information creates evidence. Theses send notifications, not evidence, so they cannot prove themselves.\n- One queue writes each thesis: the theme's queue for theme theses; the company, cryptocurrency or commodity queue for asset theses. Within an item, write themes before assets. Rejudgement order follows dependencies under the round rule. Other queues send requests, such as dependency changes or replacement links, to the owning queue with the item's sequence number.\n- Order by sequence, never the computer clock. An item reads only records from earlier sequences plus its own round's writes. The result must match serial processing. If concurrent work changes anything it read, including search lists, before commit, redo the affected work.\n- One item's changes form a round. Finish evidence links, thesis creation, succession and dependencies, then rejudge in dependency order. Dependants read conclusions freshly judged in that round. Judge each thesis once. A widely depended-on thesis such as the Fed rate triggers each dependant once only if it declares its conclusion changed; otherwise propagation stops.\n- Append-only records: questions, ladders and due dates are immutable. Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labels append records with sequence numbers.\n- The timeline only appends, never rewrites past knowledge. A late statement already superseded by a newer one is logged as superseded and unused, not added to the thesis. Previously unknown information counts normally with its true date. The evidence-matching model distinguishes these cases; add no separate rule.\n- A revised input changes only its own source reference. Revising a retelling does not change original evidence. For an original-source revision, changed statements are replaced and deleted statements marked replaced. Rejudge each open thesis linked to affected statements once. A retelling-reference change alone does not trigger rejudgement. Keep old judgement records unchanged.\n- On failure, record the item's completed step and retry from the failed step, replacing only that attempt's own result. Repeated failures go to human review.\n- Trace both directions: a thesis to all information that affected it; an item to theses it created and all evidence it sourced, including repeats.\n\nChoose storage location and database during implementation.\n<!-- /generated: order -->\n\n## Module contracts\n\n| Module | Exchange |\n|---|---|\n| Extraction | Receive sourced evidence, candidates, associations and necessary group definitions. Supply open subject theses and their dependencies: question, focus, due date and links, without states/evidence, plus available groups under this contract. |\n| Config | Read matching/rejudgement settings and search count; record the version with every call. |\n| Pull mode | Receive day-end/scheduled updates and due-date freezing/closure; remaining design is deferred. |\n| Final verdict | Whether it remains separate is deferred; due-date freezing is decided. |\n| Human review pool | Send proposed out-of-universe themes and repeated failures. Resolvable out-of-universe assets receive only processing-log entries. |\n| Matching labels | Supply every comparison record; receive human labels and, later, a smaller model. |\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Preserve extraction's associations against final thesis IDs and trace real evidence links under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction). Default integration is pending; isolated code checks do not satisfy this acceptance.\n- Every thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date; five rungs or two for yes/no, with exhaustive, non-overlapping, verifiable outcomes.\n- Numeric theses name the year and open at most one per subject/quantity for this year and next year; a previous year can remain open until due. Events have one open thesis per event. These are matching-time rules, excluding manual reopening.\n- Candidate search covers all open and closed theses, taking top N before comparison. Evidence search stays within its thesis.\n- Every comparison records shown input, raw response, Config version and decision maker. Reject destinations outside the shown lists or relevant evidence, and dependencies not shown or explicitly awaiting a chain/year, including cycles.\n- Classify every evidence comparison as repeat, replace or new. A repeat adds a reference without rejudging existing links; a new link to an open thesis triggers its rejudgement. Replaced evidence stays in history.\n- Store evidence once across multiple thesis links. Carry other links to replacement evidence.\n- Rejudge only open theses with changed inputs: new, replaced or newly linked evidence, or changed dependency conclusions. Memo-derived theses without evidence remain unjudged. Cite evidence and relevant dependency IDs, and declare conclusion change. First judgement and rung changes must declare a change. Only facts can establish which rung holds.\n- Choose dependencies during matching from shown theses or explicitly awaited chain/years. Numeric dependencies read the specified year; event dependencies follow succession. No cycles.\n- Judge dependencies before dependants and each thesis once per item round.\n- Append records without rewriting them. Trace all theses, evidence, links and matching records to their origins, and source items to created theses and sourced evidence.\n- Append knowledge in arrival order. Log obsolete late statements without adding them; accept newly learned matters with their true dates. A revised retelling changes only its own source reference. Original-source revisions replace changed statements and mark deleted ones replaced.\n- Retain every comparison; send code-refused and random samples for human labelling.\n\n## Open questions\n\n<!-- generated: open -->\n- Search method and ranking remain open: semantic, keyword or both. Test on stored information during implementation. Start with top 10 and adjust during experiments.\n- Labelling details remain open: who, how many, which interface, model evaluation and training, handover to a smaller model, and checks for omitted or mislinked extraction evidence.\n- Design pull mode later: day-end/scheduled execution, overall review, and whether final verdict remains a separate step.\n<!-- /generated: open -->\n\n## Proposals awaiting Alex's decision\n\nOther proposals were resolved individually. The due-date proposal became state freezing. These two remain for pull-mode design and must be decided before implementation.\n\n<!-- generated: proposed -->\n1. Proposal for later pull-mode design: besides due-date freezing, distinguish an unavailable answer (quantity no longer disclosed or event ceases to exist; dependants lack that input) and pending publication (period ended but the number is not yet released).\n2. Proposal for later pull-mode design: daily pairwise duplicate checks among open theses of a subject; merge the later thesis into the earlier, move links and dependencies, check cycles and rejudge. Add separate duplicate-check settings to Config.\n<!-- /generated: proposed -->\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D11/D16/D26: subject ownership, human review, central library and sources. D30: count independent original sources.\n- D31: sequence ordering and one writing queue per thesis; later decisions revise relationships, late inputs and universe removal.\n- D33: read-only extraction; library matching/writing.\n- D38–D40: question/ladder/due-date structure, state, evidence links/tiers, dependencies/succession, round-based intake, history and matching labels.\n- D41/D42: align frozen modules with the rebuilt library, show dependencies during extraction, separate evidence/candidates, compare within-thesis evidence, and permit binary ladders.\n- D44: initial model-owned identity and step-4 matching. D45: distinguish recorded decisions from proposals; similar questions remain separate. D155 later bounds code-owned identity in the POC.\n- D46/D49: one state, concrete ladders, ordinary ingestion of decisive evidence, years based on publication day, top-N whole-library search.\n- D47: consistent terms, evidence comparison after candidate resolution, dependency references in judgement reasons and Reuters example correction.\n- D48: library and matching-label freeze, October 20, 2026.\n- D51: rejudge on changed inputs, explicit conclusion-change declaration, dependency selection within matching, seven intake steps and no consensus calculation.\n- D52/D53: plain consistent wording and full context review without new rules.\n- D54: freeze the current state when due; no separate result or extra wait; pull mode owns time.\n- D55–D57: manual universe maintenance and a single open/closed switch; only open theses update; manual closure/reopening allowed.\n- D58: clarify dependency direction, waiting, due-date scope, matching-time uniqueness, triggers and manual-operation records; freeze all except final verdict/pull mode.\n- D59/D60: subject identification and personal memos; no judgement until first evidence; clarify wording and diagram without new rules.\n- D149: this module owns group handoff/storage; matching does not regroup extraction's associations.\n\n<a id=\"研究主线抽取时归组\"></a>\n\n## Research groups: associate during extraction\n\n<!-- generated: relations -->\n- D149 requirement: organise theses by the continuing business process they discuss, called a research group. Vera Rubin readiness and customer deployment can share a group while shipment dates, SK factory launch and OpenAI deployment retain separate questions, ladders and due dates. A group may start with one thesis; a thesis may belong to several groups. A group has no state or ladder, creates no automatic dependency and is not an overarching investment thesis for an agent.\n- Associate by business process, across companies and industries, not merely by company. A customer deployment explicitly using Rubin can join Rubin; NVIDIA PC chips cannot join just because they share a company. OpenAI deployment cannot join an SK-specific group merely because both involve Rubin. A group does not replace a research-universe theme.\n- Before the full-text call, code supplies available group IDs, names and scopes. Search is not restricted by company. During that call, extraction references shown groups or declares new named and scoped groups. A candidate that tracks an ongoing matter (a product rollout and its deployments, a deal, a policy process, an investment or demand cycle) cites one or more groups, each shown or declared, without repeats; a company's own reported or guided financial lines (total or segment revenue, margins, expenses, tax rate, capex) belong to the company and cite none unless the line itself tracks such a matter (D165). Code validates the references before writing, and creates a declared group only when a written thesis that tracks a matter cites it, so a company's lines alone never found a group. Declare a new group if none fits; do not force an approximate fit.\n- Matching still decides thesis/evidence identity and dependencies; it does not reread association fields to regroup. After creation, succession or merging, the writer resolves candidate IDs to final thesis IDs and local group IDs to stored group IDs, then stores membership. Candidates failing source checks or ultimately skipped do not become members. Do not create empty groups for unused declarations or reject other valid memberships because of them.\n- Store each group/member pair once, with input ID and sequence number under the library's ordering and historical-read rules. Commit groups and memberships atomically with theses and evidence; roll all back on failure. Never mark a partial write complete. Redelivery must not duplicate membership; use existing dispatch and recovery.\n- Groups do not copy evidence. Read sources through members' real evidence links, filtered by the requested sequence and excluding evidence already replaced at that point. No link means no evidence. A new member or an existing thesis change flag updates its groups. A repeated quote only adds a source and does not trigger an update. Evidence-only articles update existing memberships without inventing theses or groups.\n- Acceptance measures source-supported membership and evidence retention, not identical names, IDs or group counts on every run. Report omissions, check refusals, missed associations and wrong associations separately. Preserve Rubin shipment/customer-deployment relationships and check wrong links to unrelated products or customer-specific groups. Multi-company and large-library behaviour is not yet validated.\n- Implementation reference remains an isolated experiment: D147 supplies existing_subjects; threads declares id, name and scope; candidates[].thread_ids holds references. Local IDs are t plus a positive integer; stored IDs are g plus a positive integer. Prototype search uses title/full-text words against a global name/scope index, taking N from existing Config. These are prototype fields and search choices, not default-pipeline capabilities or evidence of large-library recall.\n- See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for gaps and steps. D148 member context and D150 reason-first output did not solve wrong grouping. D152's four name-selection responses to the third article created no candidates, so new membership was unexercised. Preserve all prior responses and results. Overarching thesis generation, MCP presentation and historical backfill are outside that implementation scope.\n<!-- /generated: relations -->\n",
 "docs/prd/07-final-verdict.md": "# Final verdict\n\n> Status: earlier draft, to be redesigned with pull mode. Due-date state freezing is decided (D54). English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> The earlier draft uses the pre-D38 library. Its verification conditions, confirmation/falsification, premises, seven-day fallback, same-tier-disagreement escalation and waiting rules no longer apply.\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Final verdict → Details. Recorded decisions take precedence.\n\n## Current decision\n\nPull mode freezes a thesis at its current rung and closes it when due. Whether a separate final-verdict step is still needed remains open. The earlier draft used a complete model review before closure.\n\n- A thesis has one state and no separate settlement judgement. Decisive information, such as annual results or an official action, enters normally as evidence and can move the state.\n- Freeze the current rung on the due date and close the thesis. Dependencies read its closing state and reasons.\n- Pull mode runs at day-end or scheduled intervals without an additional wait.\n- Example: NVDA FY2027 China results move the thesis to “down at least 80%”. Day-end pull freezes that rung and closes it.\n\n## Open question\n\nThe original rationale was to review all evidence before closure. Rejudgement now already reads all evidence still in force. Claude proposed handling closure in pull mode without a separate step; this is not approved. Retain final-verdict Config settings until the decision.\n\n## Earlier draft (D29): no longer the current contract\n\nBefore closure, the model reads the thesis, all evidence, premise states and trigger, then closes it as confirmed, falsified or expired, or keeps it open.\n\n### Original rationale\n\nAlex requested a complete review before closure. A single condition or evidence item shows only part of the picture. As with predeclared investment exit criteria, the earlier draft required predefined verification conditions and factual evidence; a larger number of opinions was insufficient.\n\n### Triggers\n\n<!-- generated: triggers -->\n| Trigger | Detected by |\n|---|---|\n| Confirmation or falsification condition met | Code |\n| Deadline reached | Pull mode, after all items published no later than the deadline have been processed |\n| Premise status changes: falsified, expired, confirmed, superseded or no longer tracked | Code, immediately |\n| Premise weakened | At the subject's next item, or within seven days via pull mode |\n| Weakening evidence arrives in the fact tier | Code |\n| Disagreement: independent sources within one tier support and weaken the thesis | Code |\n| A revised source item removes the thesis's basis | Code |\n<!-- /generated: triggers -->\n\n### Outcomes\n\n<!-- generated: verdict -->\n```\nFinal verdict | Does this thesis still hold? (The model reads the thesis, all evidence, premise states and trigger)\n├─ Confirmation condition met with factual evidence → Close: confirmed (Identify the evidence used)\n├─ Falsification condition met with factual evidence → Close: falsified (Identify the evidence used)\n├─ Deadline reached without decisive evidence → Close: expired (Record “no data to verify”, neither success nor failure)\n└─ Other cases → Keep open (May remove or replace premises and add verification conditions specifying decisive facts and when they can be known)\n```\n<!-- /generated: verdict -->\n\n### Rules\n\n<!-- generated: rules -->\n1. The model reviews the thesis, all evidence, current premises and trigger in one judgement.\n2. Only verification conditions plus factual evidence can establish confirmation or falsification. Third-party views and rumours alone cannot. Expiry is not a correctness judgement.\n3. Write a new thesis version citing evidence and sources. Retain closed theses for historical lookup.\n4. Closing a thesis sends each thesis that uses it as a premise to another final verdict.\n5. Pass “now” into pull mode: real time in production, replay time in tests. The system owns no clock.\n<!-- /generated: rules -->\n\n### Example\n\nIn August 2025, reported Commerce issuance of H20 licences meets the old falsification condition for a tightening-controls thesis. Code sends it for a complete review, and the model closes it as falsified, citing the official-action report.\n\nIts dependent NVDA thesis about China data-centre revenue approaching zero is then reviewed. Reported Chinese procurement restrictions still prevent revenue recovery, so that thesis remains with a new premise.\n\n### Earlier acceptance criteria\n\n- No closure without a final verdict.\n- Third-party views and rumours alone cannot confirm or falsify a thesis.\n- At expiry without decisive evidence, record “no data to verify”, neither success nor failure.\n- Write a new version with evidence, sources and Config version.\n- Wait until all items published no later than the deadline are processed before an expiry verdict.\n- Closing a thesis sends every premise-dependent thesis for another review.\n\n### Earlier open questions\n\n- Judge multiple triggered theses for one subject separately or in one call.\n- Validate the provisional seven-day fallback interval on data.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D14: pull mode owns scheduled and expiry work.\n- D29: earlier pre-closure review, superseded by D54 state freezing.\n- D30/D31: earlier contradictory-statement and premise-change rules, superseded by D38 dependencies.\n- D54: freeze and close at the due date without a separate result or extra wait; decide the separate step later.\n- D55/D56: label obsolete rules and their historical decision references explicitly.\n",
 "docs/prd/08-pull-mode.md": "# Pull mode\n\n> Status: scheduling and due-date freezing are decided (D54); remaining design is deferred. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> The earlier draft predates D41. Its verification conditions, premises, fallback reviews, driver merging/splitting and waiting requirements no longer apply.\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Pull mode. Recorded decisions take precedence.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe only time-driven module: run at day-end or scheduled intervals to review and update theses, and freeze and close those that are due.\n\n## Decided behaviour\n\n- The incoming stream handles items without reading the calendar; pull mode owns time-driven work.\n- Run at day-end or at scheduled intervals for an overall review and update.\n- Freeze due theses at their current rung, without a separate result. Decisive information enters normally as evidence; freezing does not wait for it.\n- Dependencies read the closing state and reasons. Further tracking uses a new successor thesis under the library's rules.\n- Supply “now” externally: real time in production, replay time in tests. The system has no internal clock.\n- Upstream delivery is distinct and must not be called pull mode.\n\n## Example\n\nSuppose NVDA FY2027 China revenue is due on the annual earnings date, illustrated as February 25, 2027. Published results show an 85% decline versus FY2026. The library rejudges it to “down at least 80%”. Day-end pull freezes that state and closes the thesis. Dependencies read the closing conclusion. An FY2028 successor continues tracking.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Detailed day-end/scheduled execution and what the overall review does.\n- Whether final verdict is separate. Claude proposed integrating it here because rejudgement already reads all evidence still in force; this is unapproved. Keep its Config settings meanwhile.\n- The library's two proposals: unavailable answers/pending publication, and daily duplicate checks.\n- Whether to fetch exact settlement numbers such as prices/XBRL, from the team's library or original source, and whether those enter through dispatch.\n\n## Earlier draft: no longer used\n\nThe former design sent due theses to a final verdict after all pre-deadline publications were processed; reviewed weakened premises after seven days; and periodically merged/split drivers. D38/D41 removed those concepts; D54 removed the extra wait.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D12/D13: the stream does not read the calendar. D14: design the two modes separately.\n- D29: earlier expiry final verdict, superseded by D54.\n- D31: externally supplied “now”; the premise-lag fallback is obsolete.\n- D54: scheduled overall review and due-date freezing without waiting.\n",
 "docs/prd/09-review-pool.md": "# Human review pool\n\n> Status: first version frozen, October 18, 2026; intake only, output deferred. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163).\n> Design page: `docs/system-design.html` → New → Human review pool. Recorded decisions take precedence.\n\n## Purpose\n\nHold items that lookup tables or rules cannot resolve for a person to review. Do not guess.\n\nClassification already requires registering unknown table entries and identifiers, while an item with unresolved subjects can continue. Without a review destination, these gaps would accumulate unnoticed.\n\n## Intake\n\n| Condition | Item handling |\n|---|---|\n| Unknown filing code or dataset | Invalid; do not process yet |\n| Unresolved author type | Invalid; do not process yet |\n| Unregistered publication series | Classify by author rules and process normally |\n| Unresolved company, cryptocurrency, commodity or theme identifier | Process normally; mark subject unresolved |\n| Candidate proposes an out-of-universe theme, such as a new conflict | Do not open the thesis; record the reason and proposed theme |\n| Repeated processing failure | Remain at the failed step for review |\n| Missing upstream information | Return |\n\n## Exclusions\n\nA resolvable company, cryptocurrency or commodity merely outside the universe does not enter the pool. For example, Samsung in Apple news may resolve in reference data without belonging to our research universe.\n\n## Records\n\nKeep one entry per issue, with occurrence count and examples, rather than one entry per input item.\n\n## Output\n\nDeferred.\n\n## Intake acceptance\n\n- Each intake condition creates the corresponding pool entry.\n- Resolvable out-of-universe assets do not enter.\n- Repeated occurrences produce one entry with accurate count and examples.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- How review updates lookup tables or the research universe and takes effect.\n- Notifications and review ownership.\n\n## Related decisions\n\n- D16: unresolved items enter human review. D19: intake only; output deferred.\n- D27/D33/D41: proposed library themes enter the pool. D31: repeated processing failures enter.\n- D37: first-version freeze; changes need a decision.\n",
 "docs/prd/README.md": "# PRD index\n\nEach module has one Markdown specification. Select a module on `docs/system-design.html`, then open its PRD link. The system PRD link at the top opens the overview.\n\n| File | Module | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 00-system.md | System overview | Main flow defined |\n| 01-universe.md | Research universe | First version frozen; specific themes remain a draft |\n| 02-classification.md | Upstream classification | First version frozen |\n| 03-config.md | Config | First version frozen |\n| 04-dispatch.md | Dispatch | First version frozen |\n| 05-extraction.md | Extraction (read-only) | Foundation frozen; groups declared during extraction in the default pipeline (D165); category prompts still need validation |\n| 05-extraction-standard.html | Extraction standard: ledger records, question types and opening, evidence and weight, golden set, prompt measurement | Signed (D243), open it in a browser; the one measuring standard for extraction, replacing `docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md` (D229) |\n| 06-views.md | Thesis library and matching records | Foundation frozen; group handoff and storage in the default pipeline (D165); other open items are in the PRD |\n| 07-final-verdict.md | Final verdict | Earlier draft, to be reconsidered with pull mode; due-date freezing is decided |\n| 08-pull-mode.md | Pull mode | Earlier draft; day-end/scheduled work and due-date freezing are decided |\n| 09-review-pool.md | Human review pool | First version frozen; intake defined, output deferred |\n\n## Maintenance rules\n\nAuthority: Alex's instructions, then `docs/DECISIONS.md`, then the PRDs and design page. If a PRD contradicts a decision, correct the PRD.\n\nAfter **every change**, reason through every module in this order: research universe → classification → Config → dispatch → extraction → thesis library → final verdict → pull mode → human review pool → system overview. Compare each with the decisions and ask:\n\n1. Does this change affect the module?\n2. Do its page and PRD describe the same behaviour?\n3. Do the examples still work?\n4. Do the acceptance criteria or open questions need to change?\n\nCorrect inconsistencies and list what changed and why. Update decisions and current state in the same slice.\n\nRun `python3 docs/check.py` **last**. It compares generated PRD blocks with page data, rebuilds the bundle used when the page opens from disk, runs the classification simulation and checks rendering. It reports mismatches rather than correcting their meaning. A pass is a mechanical result, not the reasoning review.\n"
};
